{"id": "e20562c2-9e5a-4116-8fad-5612c5195c0b", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:47:20.623723", "source": "user", "parent_id": "49bb9ac7-09fa-48e4-ad74-b1aa037d904f", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is the initial inspection phase for a design-only task. The research question (from the study context) is whether the adversarial game setup used in SPAG — imitation learning from GPT-4 followed by self-play on Adversarial Taboo episodes — yields superior reasoning improvements versus non-adversarial setups like 20-Question and Guess-My-City. The submission contract is a final experiment design (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility); execution is not required and the bubblewrap environment has no network access and no preinstalled dependencies, so do not propose running real model training or live API calls as part of this design. For now, please do the following three things and stop: (1) Inspect the repository at /workspace (list its contents, read any README or top-level docs, and summarize what is present versus missing, especially anything related to SPAG, Adversarial Taboo, 20-Question, or Guess-My-City). (2) Propose a concrete experimental design that would answer the question, covering: hypothesis (with a directional or non-directional form), independent variable (adversarial vs non-adversarial self-play), dependent variables (reasoning benchmarks you would measure, e.g., standard suites relevant to SPAG-style evaluation), control conditions (matched dataset size, compute, teacher model, prompt format, number of self-play steps), training pipeline (GPT-4 imitation + self-play loop specifics — explicitly mark which details are assumed because they are not in the provided context), and an analysis plan (statistical comparisons, effect sizes, ablations such as removing imitation or self-play). (3) Enumerate the consequential assumptions and uncertainties: which SPAG setup details you are inferring (since this conversation only states adversarial self-play via the Taboo game; reward structure, opponent sampling, episode length, and exact GPT-4 prompting are not specified and are unknowns), which reasoning benchmark to use (unknown), expected compute and runtime budget (unknown), and any other gaps. Do not produce the full final design yet — wait for my approach_review feedback.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"Does the adversarial game setup used in SPAG, which includes imitation learning of GPT-4 followed by self-play on game episodes (specifically via the adversarial Taboo game), provide superior reasoning improvements compared to non-adversarial setups like 20-Question and Guess-My-City?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [\"neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2\", \"neurips2024/93638/3:setup_32\", \"neurips2024/93638/3:setup_33\"], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
