{"id": "c39c1314-3d6e-43a0-9347-9645362db558", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:47:37.172726", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "002b21a0-257e-4328-b585-57afea04975e", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-13bfa6dc-31e5-406f-ad98-b3bb40e9889b", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"find / -name \\\"track.md\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -10; echo \\\"---\\\"; find / -iname \\\"*spag*\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -10; echo \\\"---\\\"; find / -iname \\\"*taboo*\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -10; echo \\\"---\\\"; find / -iname \\\"*neurips*\\\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head -10\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 1.318, \"output\": \"---\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/filter/ui/pdflinkspage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/svx/ui/xformspage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/twolinespage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/smarttagoptionspage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/galleryfilespage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/optfontspage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/effectspage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/numberingoptionspage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/optlanguagespage.ui\\n/lib/libreoffice/share/config/soffice.cfg/cui/ui/optpathspage.ui\\n---\\n---\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "002b21a0-257e-4328-b585-57afea04975e", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 9}
{"id": "8a0d78a4-d198-411b-ba7e-720c7113942a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:48:21.943615", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c39c1314-3d6e-43a0-9347-9645362db558", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bc9ccd99-7b09-4895-be4e-3fb173eac60e", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bc9ccd99-7b09-4895-be4e-3fb173eac60e", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Submit initial inspection + design plan\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Repository inspection — what is present vs. missing\\n\\n**Present in `/workspace`:**\\n- `/workspace/task.json` — the task descriptor. It contains the research question (whether SPAG-style adversarial self-play via Taboo beats non-adversarial games 20-Question and Guess-My-City for reasoning), a short context (\\\"design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"; \\\"researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic\\\"; \\\"do not claim that an experiment was run\\\"), and pointers to three researcher topics: `neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33`.\\n- No README, no code, no datasets, no SPAG paper text, no tracked study artifacts. No `/workspace` subdirectories beyond `task.json`.\\n\\n**Missing (would normally be needed but not provided):**\\n- SPAG paper or excerpt (imitation size, self-play recipe, opponent sampling, reward, base student model, evaluation suite).\\n- The supplementary/note content behind the topic IDs `setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33` — these are the only handles the task gives me to ground assumptions in source text, and I cannot read them.\\n- Any reference implementation of Taboo / 20-Question / Guess-My-City prompts, scoring rules, or word lists.\\n- Reasoning benchmark selection guidance.\\n- Compute / runtime budget.\\n\\n**Consequence:** essentially all SPAG operational details (reward, opponent sampling, episode length, prompts, optimizer, base model, imitation corpus size) must be **treated as assumptions** rather than facts, and explicitly flagged as such in the design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed experimental design\\n\\n### Research question\\nDoes the SPAG pipeline (GPT-4 imitation learning → adversarial self-play on the Taboo game) produce larger downstream reasoning gains than the same pipeline but with non-adversarial self-play on 20-Question or Guess-My-City?\\n\\n### Hypothesis\\n- **Directional form (H1):** Taboo (adversarial) > 20-Question > Guess-My-City for downstream reasoning accuracy, holding constant pipeline compute, model capacity, imitation volume, and prompt structure.\\n- **Null (H0):** Δ-accuracy on the chosen reasoning benchmark(s) is statistically indistinguishable across the three self-play regimes.\\n- Non-directional backup H1 (covered by omnibus test): at least one regime differs; post-hoc contrasts locate where.\\n\\n### Independent variable\\n- **Self-play environment**, 3 levels:\\n  1. **Adversarial** → SPAG-style Taboo (describer vs. guesser with competing incentives: communicate target while avoiding taboo words).\\n  2. **Information-seeking, non-adversarial cooperative** → 20-Question (one player asks, one answers toward a shared hidden answer; no opposing side).\\n  3. **Information-seeking, no opponent** → Guess-My-City (single agent narrows a hidden city; no adversary or counter-player).\\n\\n*(Open question for the researcher: SPAG literature sometimes treats 20-Question as zero-sum between guesser's information gain and answerer's entropy; please confirm which framing is source-supported. I will assume the cooperative framing unless told otherwise.)*\\n\\n### Dependent variables\\n- **Primary:** downstream reasoning accuracy on a standard suite — recommended default **BIG-Bench Hard (BBH)** (coincides with SPAG's own reported benchmark per the paper I recall), plus at least one math/logic suite (**GSM8K** or **MATH**) and one general-knowledge reasoning suite (**MMLU-STEM / ARC-Challenge**). All evaluated zero-shot with fixed prompts.\\n- **Secondary (in-game, diagnostic only):** episode success rate; mean turns-to-success; (for Taboo) taboo-violation rate; (for Q20) average information gain per turn.\\n- Reported as **delta from the post-imitation checkpoint** to isolate the *self-play* contribution from the *imitation* contribution.\\n\\n### Control conditions (matched)\\nIdentical across the three regimes:\\n- Same base student model (assumed: an open small/medium LM, e.g., Llama-3-8B-class; configurable, see assumptions).\\n- Equal **N_imitation** GPT-4 trajectories per game.\\n- Equal **N_selfplay** episodes and **same wall-clock training compute** (FLOP-matched, not just step-matched).\\n- Identical prompt template scaffolding and token budget per turn; game-specific content (target word, taboo list, city pool) swapped in.\\n- Identical optimizer hyperparameters, batch size, learning rate schedule, and seed count (**≥3 seeds**).\\n- Identical evaluation harness, few-shot counts, and decoding settings.\\n\\n### Ablations (factorial)\\nA 3 × 2 matrix (game ∈ {Taboo, Q20, City} × stage ∈ {imitation only, imitation + self-play}), plus a no-game baseline:\\n| Condition | Imitation | Self-play |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Taboo-impl-only | ✅ | — |\\n| Taboo-full (SPAG) | ✅ | ✅ |\\n| Q20-impl-only  | ✅ | — |\\n| Q20-full       | ✅ | ✅ |\\n| City-impl-only | ✅ | — |\\n| City-full      | ✅ | ✅ |\\n| Baseline (instruct-only FT, no game) | — | — |\\n\\nThis isolates (a) game effect given self-play, (b) self-play effect given game, and (c) interaction.\\n\\n### Training pipeline (assumed details called out)\\n1. **Imitation stage.** Student LM is SFT'd on GPT-4-played trajectories in each game. **Assumption:** ~10k trajectories per game unless the researcher overrides; GPT-4 prompting template reused across games; CoT-style reasoning traces are kept in the trajectory. *(Assumed — not in supplied context.)*\\n2. **Self-play stage.** Post-imitation checkpoint self-plays against a periodically-snapshotted copy of itself (or a heuristic opponent for the cooperative regimes). Reward: +1 on win, 0 otherwise for Taboo; +1 on correct identification within turn budget for Q20 and City. Update via **rejection-sampling fine-tuning** on self-play winners (lightweight, BR-style), with PPO noted as an alternative. **Assumption:** opponent snapshot every K episodes; if SPAG used a different scheme (PSRO league, replay buffer, frozen-final opponent), flag in design. *(Assumed.)*\\n3. **Evaluation.** Every checkpoint evaluated on the held-out reasoning suite plus a held-out game split to detect overfitting to in-game reward.\\n\\n### Analysis plan\\n- **Omnibus:** mixed-effects model with `condition` (7 levels incl. ablations) × `benchmark` as fixed effects, `seed` and `model_id` as random effects.\\n- **Planned contrasts:**\\n  - Taboo-full vs. Q20-full, Taboo-full vs. City-full (primary).\\n  - Full vs. impl-only within each game (self-play gain).\\n  - Full vs. baseline (does any game help at all).\\n- **Correction:** Holm–Bonferroni across the 3 primary contrasts; report adjusted p.\\n- **Effect size:** Cohen's d for paired across-seed differences, with 95% bootstrap CIs.\\n- **Power:** With ≥3 seeds and matched FLOPs, the design aims to detect d ≈ 0.5; if you need finer resolution, increase to ≥5 seeds (still cheap relative to a single training run).\\n- **Diagnostics:** learning curves on game success vs. benchmark accuracy; correlation between in-game reward and downstream benchmark — this is the key mechanistic check (does adversarial pressure predict reasoning gain?).\\n\\n### Feasibility checks (design-level, not execution)\\n- Per-condition FLOPs estimated from (N_imitation × model params × tokens) + (N_selfplay × episode length × 2 × model params).\\n- Implementation risk: prompt leakage between games → mitigated by disjoint content pools (Taboo words, city lists, target-object set) and disjoint evaluation splits.\\n- Contamination risk: reasoning benchmark content leaking into GPT-4's self-play prompts → mitigated by **strict separation** of game content and benchmark content (the design forbids sharing corpora).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n### SPAG operational details inferred (not in supplied context)\\n- **Reward structure in Taboo**: assumed sparse +1/−1 (win/loss) with tabular-word violation tracked as auxiliary metric. Source-provided value would replace.\\n- **Opponent sampling in self-play**: assumed snapshot-self every K episodes. Source may specify PSRO, league, replay-buffer, or ELO-curated opponents — please confirm.\\n- **Episode length / max turns**: not specified; assumed ≈20 turns cap.\\n- **Exact GPT-4 prompting template**: assumed CoT-enabled, system prompt instructing strategic play. Not specified.\\n- **Imitation corpus size**: assumed ~10k trajectories per game. Source may differ by 1–2 orders of magnitude.\\n- **Self-play optimizer**: assumed rejection-sampling fine-tuning (cheapest, defensible); SPAG may have used PPO or DPO. Needs confirmation.\\n- **Base student model**: assumed open small/medium LM; SPAG used a specific one. Please name if known.\\n- **Whether 20-Question is treated as adversarial in SPAG**: this is the pivotal ambiguity — affects interpretation of \\\"non-adversarial.\\\" Researcher topic `setup_2` / `setup_32` / `setup_33` may clarify.\\n\\n### Reasoning benchmark choice (unknown)\\n- BBH is the conventional SPAG-aligned default. Alternatives: GSM8K, MATH, ARC, MMLU. **Will not commit to one without researcher sign-off**, and recommend the design include ≥2 suites so the result is not benchmark-specific.\\n\\n### Compute and runtime budget (unknown)\\n- bubblewrap env explicitly has no GPU, no network, no preinstalled deps, and the task contract is *design-only* — no live training is proposed. The design budgets training compute in FLOPs and asks the researcher to fix the envelope (e.g., \\\"one full pipeline run fits in ≤X H100-hours\\\").\\n\\n### Other gaps\\n- Whether SPAG reports **paired seed-level results** (needed for proper within-run paired tests) vs. only point estimates.\\n- Whether the paper ablates opponent strength / opponent diversity — if so, the design should mirror that protocol rather than reinvent it.\\n- Whether \\\"reasoning improvement\\\" in the question is a single-benchmark claim or a suite-average claim; if the former, the benchmark must be named.\\n\\n### Ask to proceed\\nPlease confirm or correct, in priority order:\\n1. The framing of 20-Question (cooperative vs. adversarial). *(Highest-impact assumption.)*\\n2. The base student model and reasoning benchmark suite to evaluate on.\\n3. The self-play optimizer used in SPAG (RSFT / PPO / DPO / other) and opponent-sampling scheme.\\n4. Imitation corpus size per game, if known.\\n5. Compute envelope you'd commit to (so we can quote a feasibility number in the final design).\\n\\nOnce these are settled I will produce the full final experiment design (stage=result) with rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility check — but I will **not** fabricate outcomes or pretend to execute anything in the bubblewrap env.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository inspection (executed): (a) `ls -la /workspace` returns a single file `task.json` plus parent directories; (b) `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` returns exactly one file `task.json`; (c) the body of `task.json` was read and contains the research question, a \\\"design-only task\\\" context, and three researcher topic IDs `neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33` whose source content was not present in the workspace; (d) `find / -iname \\\"*spag*\\\"`, `find / -iname \\\"*taboo*\\\"`, and `find / -name \\\"track.md\\\"` confirmed there are no SPAG/Taboo artifacts and no `track.md` file (only libreoffice .ui filename matches are unrelated to SPAG). The bubblewrap environment listing (`runtime: bubblewrap, network: disabled, gpu_enabled: false, cpu_affinity_count: 24, software_environment: system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled, datasets: []`) is taken directly from the task contract — no actual training, network calls, or model inference is feasible or task-required (the contract explicitly says do not claim an experiment was run).\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Compute envelope: not specified — design quotes it in FLOPs and asks researcher to fix.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Reasoning evaluation suite: BBH as primary (SPAG-aligned default) plus GSM8K/MATH and MMLU/ARC as secondary — proposed, not mandated.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"20-Question is treated as cooperative / non-adversarial in the contrast — pivotal assumption requiring researcher confirmation.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"GPT-4 teacher prompting: CoT-enabled system prompt instructing strategic play — inferred.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Episode length cap: ~20 turns — inferred.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Taboo reward: sparse +1/-1 with taboo-word violation tracked as auxiliary metric — inferred.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Opponent sampling during self-play: periodic snapshot of the policy itself — inferred.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Self-play optimizer: rejection-sampling fine-tuning on self-play winners; PPO/DPO flagged as alternatives — inferred.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Imitation corpus size: ~10k GPT-4 trajectories per game — inferred default.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Base student model: open small/medium LM, around Llama-3-8B class — inferred, not stated.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether \\\"reasoning improvement\\\" in the research question is a single-benchmark claim or a multi-suite claim.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Whether SPAG's reported numbers are averages over seeds or single-run point estimates (affects feasibility of paired statistical tests).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Compute / wall-clock budget available to the