{"id": "b13ece55-2725-497b-917f-6984a89803df", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:48:50.212642", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "12d9272d-622a-4ab3-ac25-c19627730dd0", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## (1) Repository inspection — what is present vs. missing\n\n**Present in `/workspace`:**\n- `/workspace/task.json` — the task descriptor. It contains the research question (whether SPAG-style adversarial self-play via Taboo beats non-adversarial games 20-Question and Guess-My-City for reasoning), a short context (\"design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"; \"researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic\"; \"do not claim that an experiment was run\"), and pointers to three researcher topics: `neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33`.\n- No README, no code, no datasets, no SPAG paper text, no tracked study artifacts. No `/workspace` subdirectories beyond `task.json`.\n\n**Missing (would normally be needed but not provided):**\n- SPAG paper or excerpt (imitation size, self-play recipe, opponent sampling, reward, base student model, evaluation suite).\n- The supplementary/note content behind the topic IDs `setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33` — these are the only handles the task gives me to ground assumptions in source text, and I cannot read them.\n- Any reference implementation of Taboo / 20-Question / Guess-My-City prompts, scoring rules, or word lists.\n- Reasoning benchmark selection guidance.\n- Compute / runtime budget.\n\n**Consequence:** essentially all SPAG operational details (reward, opponent sampling, episode length, prompts, optimizer, base model, imitation corpus size) must be treated as assumptions rather than facts, and explicitly flagged as such in the design.\n\n---\n\n## (2) Proposed experimental design\n\n### Research question\nDoes the SPAG pipeline (GPT-4 imitation learning → adversarial self-play on the Taboo game) produce larger downstream reasoning gains than the same pipeline but with non-adversarial self-play on 20-Question or Guess-My-City?\n\n### Hypothesis\n- **Directional form (H1):** Taboo (adversarial) > 20-Question > Guess-My-City for downstream reasoning accuracy, holding constant pipeline compute, model capacity, imitation volume, and prompt structure.\n- **Null (H0):** Δ-accuracy on the chosen reasoning benchmark(s) is statistically indistinguishable across the three self-play regimes.\n- Non-directional backup H1 (covered by omnibus test): at least one regime differs; post-hoc contrasts locate where.\n\n### Independent variable\nSelf-play environment, 3 levels:\n1. **Adversarial** — SPAG-style Taboo (describer vs. guesser with competing incentives: communicate target while avoiding taboo words).\n2. **Information-seeking, non-adversarial cooperative** — 20-Question (one player asks, one answers toward a shared hidden answer; no opposing side).\n3. **Information-seeking, no opponent** — Guess-My-City (single agent narrows a hidden city; no adversary or counter-player).\n\nThe framing of 20-Question is the pivotal ambiguity: SPAG may treat it as zero-sum (open in supplied context). Plan assumes cooperative unless the researcher overrides.\n\n### Dependent variables\n- **Primary:** downstream reasoning accuracy on a standard suite — recommended default **BIG-Bench Hard (BBH)** (coincides with SPAG's own reported benchmark), plus at least one math/logic suite (**GSM8K** or **MATH**) and one general-knowledge reasoning suite (**MMLU-STEM / ARC-Challenge**). All evaluated zero-shot with fixed prompts.\n- **Secondary (in-game, diagnostic):** episode success rate; mean turns-to-success; (for Taboo) taboo-violation rate; (for Q20) average information gain per turn.\n- Reported as **delta from the post-imitation checkpoint** to isolate the self-play contribution from the imitation contribution.\n\n### Control conditions (matched)\nIdentical across the three regimes: same base student model; equal N_imitation GPT-4 trajectories per game; equal N_selfplay episodes; identical wall-clock training compute (FLOP-matched, not just step-matched); identical prompt template scaffolding and per-turn token budget; identical optimizer hyperparameters, batch size, learning-rate schedule, and ≥3 seeds; identical evaluation harness, few-shot counts, and decoding settings.\n\n### Ablations (factorial)\nA 7-cell design (3 games × 2 stages, plus a no-game baseline):\n- Taboo-impl-only, Taboo-full (SPAG).\n- Q20-impl-only, Q20-full.\n- City-impl-only, City-full.\n- Baseline (instruct-only FT, no game).\n\nIsolates (a) game effect given self-play, (b) self-play effect given game, (c) interaction.\n\n### Training pipeline (assumed details called out)\n1. **Imitation stage.** Student LM is SFT'd on GPT-4-played trajectories in each game. Assumed ~10k trajectories per game unless overridden; CoT-style reasoning traces are preserved; prompt template reused across games.\n2. **Self-play stage.** Post-imitation checkpoint self-plays against a periodically-snapshotted copy of itself (or a heuristic opponent in cooperative regimes). Reward: +1 on win for Taboo; +1 on correct identification within turn budget for Q20 and City. Update via rejection-sampling fine-tuning on self-play winners (RSFT), with PPO noted as alternative. Assumed opponent snapshot every K episodes; if SPAG uses PSRO league / replay buffer / ELO-curated opponents, will mirror that instead.\n3. **Evaluation.** Every checkpoint is evaluated on the held-out reasoning suite and a held-out game split to detect in-game reward overfitting.\n\n### Analysis plan\n- **Omnibus:** mixed-effects model with condition (7 levels incl. ablations) × benchmark as fixed effects; seed and model_id as random effects.\n- **Planned contrasts:** Taboo-full vs. Q20-full, Taboo-full vs. City-full (primary); Full vs. impl-only within each game (self-play gain); Full vs. baseline (does any game help).\n- **Multiple-comparison correction:** Holm–Bonferroni across the 3 primary contrasts.\n- **Effect size:** Cohen's d for paired across-seed differences with 95% bootstrap CIs.\n- **Power:** with ≥3 seeds and matched FLOPs, design targets d ≈ 0.5; increase to ≥5 seeds for finer resolution.\n- **Mechanistic diagnostic:** correlation between in-game reward trajectory and downstream benchmark accuracy — does adversarial pressure actually predict reasoning gain?\n\n### Feasibility checks (design-level)\n- FLOPs estimated from (N_imitation × params × tokens) + (N_selfplay × episode length × 2 × params).\n- Prompt-leakage mitigation: disjoint content pools for each game (Taboo words, city lists, target-object set); disjoint eval splits.\n- Benchmark-contamination mitigation: strict separation of game content corpora from reasoning-benchmark corpora.\n\n---\n\n## (3) Assumptions and uncertainties\n\nWill be enumerated in full in the next turn. Headline items below.\n\n**Top assumptions (each flagged in the design as inferred, not sourced):**\n- Taboo reward: sparse +1/−1 with taboo-word violation tracked as auxiliary metric.\n- Self-play opponent: periodic self-snapshot; optimizer RSFT.\n- Episode cap: ~20 turns.\n- GPT-4 teacher prompting: CoT-enabled system prompt instructing strategic play.\n- Imitation corpus: ~10k trajectories per game.\n- Base student model: open small/medium LM, ~Llama-3-8B class.\n- 20-Question treated as cooperative / non-adversarial — pivotal assumption awaiting researcher sign-off.\n- Default reasoning suite: BBH + GSM8K (or MATH) + ARC/MMLU.\n- Compute envelope unknown; will be expressed in FLOPs.\n\n**Top uncertainties (require researcher clarification via topic IDs or direct reply):**\n- Exact SPAG reward, opponent sampling, episode length, prompts, optimizer, base model, imitation size (researcher topics `setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33` may carry this; not accessible from here).\n- Benchmark definition of \"reasoning improvement\": single benchmark or suite average?\n- Compute budget (missing entirely).\n- Whether SPAG reports seed-paired resu