{"id": "ca4b37c1-b2ab-4455-8404-9241edafbdbb", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:49:16.269182", "source": "user", "parent_id": "4146549a-7f7d-4f82-b92b-914628c8e19f", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with the following resolutions to your priority questions, plus a few guardrails. Produce the full final experiment design (no execution claims) in your next turn, flagging every inferred SPAG detail as an explicit assumption block and never as a sourced fact. The researcher_topics list provided to this task is empty, so I cannot give you the bodies of setup_2 / setup_32 / setup_33; you must continue to mark all SPAG operational specifics as inferred and not source-anchored, and the design must be robust to reasonable variations in those specifics (i.e., state them as parameters the design fixes, rather than facts about the paper). Treat any value I list below as a design choice, not a citation of SPAG text, and call it out as such in an Assumptions section. Resolutions, in your priority order: (1) 20-Question framing — fix it as **non-adversarial / cooperative**: a single asymmetric role pair (one role asks, the other answers truthfully toward a common hidden target); no opposing side and no penalty for information disclosure. This is the contrast the original question is asking about (adversarial Taboo vs two non-adversarial information-seeking games), so do not let 20-Question become adversarial in your pipeline. Note this explicitly as the design's interpretation of 'non-adversarial' and as an assumption. (2) Base student model and benchmark — design should be **model-family-agnostic but parameterize it**: specify a small open LM as default (e.g., Llama-3-8B-Instruct or an equivalently sized Qwen2.5-7B variant) and run two sizes (7B against 13B) as a scaling probe if budget permits; if neither is available in the eventual runtime, the design should still be re-parameterizable by changing one constant. Primary reasoning suite: **BIG-Bench Hard (BBH)** as the headline number, with **GSM8K** for multi-step arithmetic reasoning, **ARC-Challenge** for general scientific reasoning, and **MMLU-STEM** as a secondary general check, all zero-shot with locked prompts. Report per-benchmark and macro-averaged deltas relative to the post-imitation checkpoint. (3) Self-play optimizer and opponent sampling — default to **Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT)** on self-play winners from a frozen self-snapshot, with a periodic snapshot every K episodes (treat K as a sweep parameter with a stated default); add a **league / replay-buffer variant** as an optional arm that mirrors whatever SPAG does at the protocol level. PPO should be discussed as an alternative arm, not the default. (4) Imitation corpus size — default **10,000 GPT-4 trajectories per game**, with a smaller 2,500-trajectory arm as an ablation to probe data efficiency. (5) Compute envelope — unknown; express all sizing statements in FLOPs and GPU-hours for a single 8xA100-80G node, and include a coarse budget table showing the cost of the 7-cell factorial vs a reduced 3-cell (full games only) for stretch and bare-minimum modes. Additional guardrails for the final design: (a) Elevated Brier-style concern — 20-Question is often called 'adversarial' in some papers because the asker/guesser roles have asymmetric information; be explicit in your design that under your fixed cooperative framing, both Q20 and Guess-My-City have **no adversary and no zero-sum incentive**, which is what makes them 'non-adversarial' in the SPAG setting; (b) include a **prompt-leakage audit** ensuring GPT-4 cannot surface Taboo targets, Q20 objects, or cities that materially overlap with BBH/GSM8K/ARC/MMLU items; (c) include **mechanistic diagnostics**: correlation between in-game reward trajectory and downstream benchmark deltas (does adversarial pressure actually predict reasoning gain?), turns-to-success, and for Taboo specifically the taboo-violation rate; (d) pre-register the omnibus model as a **mixed-effects model** with condition (7 levels of the ablation grid) as fixed effect and seed and model_id as random effects, with Holm–Bonferroni over three primary contrasts (Taboo-full vs Q20-full, Taboo-full vs City-full, Q20-full vs City-full) and Cohen's d with bootstrap 95% CIs; (e) include a **feasibility and threat-to-validity subsection** covering FLOPs, seeds (≥3, target 5), contamination risk, and Rubin's rules if you later pool model sizes; (f) deliver a single self-contained final design document with sections: Background and Question, Hypotheses, Independent/Dependent Variables, Pipeline (Imitation + Self-Play), 7-Cell Ablation Grid, Controls and Matching, Evaluation Suite, Statistical Analysis Plan with α and power, Compute and Feasibility, Assumptions (every inferred SPAG detail enumerated), Threats to Validity, and Pre-registration Checklist. Stop after delivering this document. Do not fabricate outcomes, do not claim runs, do not invoke network calls, and do not import SPAG paper text you have not actually read.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
{"id": "a4923787-5e75-4839-aec9-427fd3b6c8b4", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:50:56.558354", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "ca4b37c1-b2ab-4455-8404-9241edafbdbb", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-f4301a73-bb52-4877-a576-3c0fa2e39b46", "tool_call": {"id": "call-f4301a73-bb52-4877-a576-3c0fa2e39b46", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Deliver full final experiment design document\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Final Experiment Design — SPAG Adversarial vs Non-Adversarial Self-Play for Reasoning\\n\\n**Status:** Design-only. No execution is claimed, no model weights are produced, no network or model APIs are invoked. All operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters and labelled as assumptions; the design is robust to reasonable variations in those parameters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Operating frame: what \\\"non-adversarial\\\" means in this design\\n\\nPer locked design choice, **20-Question and Guess-My-City have no adversary and no zero-sum incentive**. Specifically:\\n\\n- **20-Question:** a single asymmetric role pair — the *asker* elicits and the *answerer* answers truthfully toward a common hidden target. The answerer is rewarded for helping the asker succeed; there is no opposing side penalizing information disclosure. Success = asker identifies the target within the turn budget.\\n- **Guess-My-City:** a single agent that narrows a hidden city through its own questions/guesses. No counter-player, no opponent utility.\\n- **Taboo (SPAG):** an asymmetric role pair with opposing incentives — the describer must convey a target word *without* uttering any item from a taboo list issued in the prompt, while a guesser attempts to name the target. The taboo-avoidance constraint creates a zero-sum pressure against surface forms.\\n\\n**Assumption (locked):** Under this framing, \\\"adversarial\\\" is the Taboo condition alone; the contrast Taboo vs. {Q20, City} is the SPAG-vs-non-adversarial contrast the question asks about. If SPAG's own framing of 20-Question is zero-sum, that fact is not in the supplied context and therefore does not change this design — it would simply mean the published paper's contrast and the question's named contrast use different operationalizations, which is itself a result-of-interest and is reported as a framing discrepancy in §11 (Threats to Validity).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Background and Question\\n\\n**Background.** SPAG's pipeline, as described in the supplied task context, has two stages: (i) **imitation learning** of GPT-4 on game trajectories, and (ii) **self-play** on game episodes, with the *Adversarial Taboo* game being the proposed source of \\\"adversarial pressure\\\". The hypothesis under test is that the adversarial structure of Taboo uniquely transfers to general reasoning. Non-adversarial self-play environments (information-seeking but non-zero-sum, namely 20-Question and Guess-My-City) allow the contribution of self-play per se versus adversarial pressure per se to be separated.\\n\\n**Question.** Does SPAG (imitation + adversarial Taboo self-play) yield larger downstream reasoning gains than imitation + non-adversarial self-play on 20-Question or Guess-My-City, holding all other variables fixed?\\n\\n**Why this matters.** A positive answer implicates *adversarial pressure* as the active ingredient rather than self-play generally, with implications for which game families are worth investing in for downstream reasoning. A null answer implicates self-play *per se* (or imitation alone) as the active ingredient.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Hypotheses\\n\\n- **H0 (omnibus null):** Under FLOP-matched training, downstream reasoning Δ-accuracy is the same across the three self-play regimes (Taboo-full, Q20-full, City-full).\\n- **H1a (primary, directional):** Taboo-full > Q20-full and Taboo-full > City-full on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\\n- **H1b (secondary, directional):** Q20-full > City-full.\\n- **H0-imitation:** Imitation-only checkpoints (Taboo-impl, Q20-impl, City-impl) do not differ across games on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\\n- **H0-selfplay:** Within each game, the Full vs. Impl-only delta is zero.\\n- **H0-game:** The no-game baseline is indistinguishable from any Impl-only condition.\\n\\nThe primary inference targets H1a; everything else is a supporting contrast.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Independent and Dependent Variables\\n\\n### Independent variable (single primary)\\n- **Self-play environment**, 7-cell ablation grid (§5). The 3-cell omnibus contrast (full only) is the primary subset.\\n\\n### Dependent variables (locked)\\n\\n**Downstream reasoning (primary, locked-prompt, zero-shot):**\\n- **BBH** (BIG-Bench Hard) — headline number; macro-averaged over the 23 subtasks.\\n- **GSM8K** — multi-step arithmetic reasoning; reports strict-match accuracy.\\n- **ARC-Challenge** — general scientific reasoning; reports accuracy.\\n- **MMLU-STEM** — secondary general check (physics, chemistry, biology, math, CS, engineering); reports accuracy.\\n\\nReport per-benchmark and **macro-averaged** deltas (mean of the four per-benchmark normalized deltas).\\n\\n**In-game diagnostics (mechanistic, secondary):**\\n- Episode success rate (per game).\\n- Mean turns-to-success.\\n- Taboo-violation rate (fraction of describer episodes in which ≥1 taboo word was uttered) — diagnostic of whether Taboo produces the intended adversarial pressure.\\n- Self-play reward trajectory across self-play steps.\\n\\n**Reporting convention:** All downstream results are reported as Δ from the **post-imitation** checkpoint of the corresponding game. This isolates the self-play contribution from the imitation contribution. An \\\"absolute\\\" report (vs. base model) is included alongside for each condition because the literature often expects both.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Pipeline (Imitation + Self-Play)\\n\\n### 4.1 Phase A — Imitation\\n- **Teacher:** GPT-4 (locked).\\n- **Default corpus:** N_imitation = **10,000 trajectories per game**, with a smaller **N=2,500** arm as a data-efficiency ablation (§5).\\n- **Generation mechanism (assumed default):** GPT-4 plays both sides of each episode (or both roles for Q20/City) under a system prompt encouraging CoT-style strategic reasoning; the student's role transcript is the training target. **Cross-game prompt template reuse** — same scaffolding (system instructions, turn markers), with only the game-specific content (target word + taboo list / hidden object / hidden city) varied. This is the principal control for prompt-format confounds.\\n- **Student optimisation (assumed default):** standard SFT / next-token cross-entropy; 2–3 epochs over the corpus; AdamW; cosine LR schedule; peak LR ∈ {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5} swept (single shared sweep across cells).\\n- **Output:** one post-imitation checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, model_id, seed).\\n\\n### 4.2 Phase B — Self-Play\\n- **Default optimiser:** **Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT)** — keep winning trajectories, fine-tune on them with cross-entropy. Frozen-snapshot opponent.\\n- **Opponent-sampling default:** frozen copy of the student, snapshotted every **K episodes** (default **K=500**; sweep over K ∈ {100, 500, 2000} as a sensitivity probe).\\n- **Optional arm:** **League / replay-buffer** opponent pool (ensemble of past snapshots with ELO-curated sampling) — mirrors the protocol-level family of designs that SPAG-style papers sometimes use.\\n- **Alternative arm (not default):** PPO with the same frozen-snapshot opponent and a learned value head; included only to verify the optimizer choice is not the bottleneck for the H1a conclusion.\\n- **Reward signals (assumed defaults):**\\n  - Taboo: +1 if guesser names the target before turn cap; 0 otherwise. Taboo-word violations are tracked as an auxiliary diagnostic metric, not as a reward term (i.e., the reward is end-of-episode outcome).\\n  - 20-Question: +1 if asker names the hidden target within turn cap; partial credit ±0.25 per quartile of information gain is allowed but is excluded from the default reward and reported only as a sensitivity arm.\\n  - Guess-My-City: +1 if the agent names the hidden city within turn cap.\\n- **Episode cap:** T = 20 turns per game (default); sweep T ∈ {10, 20, 40} as a sensitivity arm.\\n- **Episode budget per cell:** N_selfplay = **20,000 episodes** (default); RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes on the rolling winner pool (10 SFT passes per cell, locked across cells).\\n- **Output:** one post-self-play checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, seed, model_id).\\n\\n### 4.3 Evaluation phase\\n- Every checkpoint is evaluated on (a) the locked downstream reasoning suite and (b) a held-out *game* split (5,000 episodes per game, distinct content from any training/trajectory content) to monitor in-game overfitting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. 7-Cell Ablation Grid\\n\\nThe factorial below is the pre-registered primary design. Cells are matched on FLOPs, prompts, optimiser (RSFT default), base model, and seeds.\\n\\n| # | Cell | Imitation | Self-play | Game | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Taboo-impl | ✅ | — | Taboo | isolates imitation contribution |\\n| 2 | **Taboo-full (SPAG)** | ✅ | ✅ | Taboo | **primary condition** |\\n| 3 | Q20-impl | ✅ | — | 20-Question | |\\n| 4 | Q20-full | ✅ | ✅ | 20-Question | primary non-adversarial #1 |\\n| 5 | City-impl | ✅ | — | Guess-My-City | |\\n| 6 | City-full | ✅ | ✅ | Guess-My-City | primary non-adversarial #2 |\\n| 7 | Baseline | — | — | — | generic instruct-only FT (no game); same SFT recipe, same FLOPs, same data volume |\\n\\n**Reduced (bare-minimum) mode:** cells {2, 4, 6} only — three full-game cells, no ablation cells. Used if budget cannot stretch to the full factorial.\\n\\n**Stretch (if compute permits):** add an 8th and 9th cell using the **N=2,500 imitation arm** at the post-self-play step (Taboo-full-2.5K, Q20-full-2.5K, City-full-2.5K) to probe data efficiency; and a 10th cell using the **League opponent** arm at Taboo-full.\\n\\n**Pre-registered primary contrasts (3):**\\n1. Taboo-full vs. Q20-full (Taboo − Q20 on macro-averaged Δ).\\n2. Taboo-full vs. City-full (Taboo − City).\\n3. Q20-full vs. City-full (Q20 − City).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Controls and Matching\\n\\nThe match-the-stuff list. Every item below is fixed identically across all cells unless explicitly varied as a cell-defining axis.\\n\\n- **Base model:** family-agnostic; default `Llama-3-8B-Instruct` (or an equivalent-size `Qwen2.5-7B-(-Instruct)` if Llama-3 unavailable). All cells use the same base checkpoint. Scaling probe: re-run **Taboo-full, Q20-full, City-full** at a 13B-class checkpoint (e.g., `Llama-3-13B-Instruct` or `Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct`) under the same protocol, treated as a model_id random effect.\\n- **Compute:** FLOP-matched. Each cell's total training FLOPs (Phase A + Phase B) is constrained to be within ±2% across cells. Concretely: if Taboo requires more tokens per self-play episode due to taboo-list conditioning, either (a) extend Q20/City episodes to the same token budget or (b) cap Taboo self-play episodes so its total tokens match. Default: option (a) — equalize per-episode token budget; report sensitivity under option (b).\\n- **Imitation data:** N_imitation matched across game cells (10,000 default).\\n- **Self-play episodes:** matched (20,000 default) and matched per-cell FLOPs on top.\\n- **Optimizer hyperparameters:** AdamW, cosine LR, identical peak LR (swept once jointly, not per cell); identical warm-up; identical batch size; identical gradient clipping; identical precision (bf16/fp32 mixed).\\n- **Prompts:** identical scaffolding; game-specific content swapped. The system-prompt token budget is matched to within ±5%.\\n- **Decoding during evaluation:** temperature 0, max-new-tokens cap, identical for all cells.\\n- **Few-shot settings:** zero-shot on all four downstream suites; locked.\\n- **Seeds:** ≥3, target **5**, identically seeded across cells (paired seed design).\\n- **Game-content pools:**\\n  - Taboo word pool: ≥5,000 distinct (target, taboo-list) pairs, disjoint from any reasoning-benchmark text.\\n  - Q20 object pool: ≥5,000 distinct common-noun targets, disjoint from any reasoning-benchmark text.\\n  - City pool: ≥5,000 cities not appearing in any reasoning-benchmark text or in BBH subtask instances.\\n  - See §10 (Prompt-leakage audit).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Evaluation Suite\\n\\nLocked prompt suite for downstream evaluation. Every cell is evaluated under identical prompts, with the prompt templates and decoding settings versioned and stored alongside the design document.\\n\\n- **BBH:** all 23 subtasks; the canonical BBH evaluation prompt for each subtask; aggregate via unweighted macro-average across subtasks. Report per-subtask score in the supplementary appendix.\\n- **GSM8K:** canonical 8-shot prompt (locked) **OR** zero-shot CoT with locked prompt — the design fixes zero-shot and locks the CoT template; the 8-shot version is reported as a sensitivity number if pre-registered capacity allows.\\n- **ARC-Challenge:** test split; zero-shot; canonical prompt.\\n- **MMLU-STEM:** zero-shot; subject-level scores aggregated to MMLU-STEM by unweighted macro-average.\\n\\n**Reporting metric:** Δ-accuracy = (post-self-play score) − (post-imitation score) for the same (game, seed). Macro-averaged Δ = mean of the four per-benchmark Δs.\\n\\n**Held-out game split:** 5,000 episodes per game, content disjoint from training/trajectory content; evaluated for episode success rate, mean turns-to-success, and (Taboo) taboo-violation rate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Statistical Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 8.1 Pre-registered model\\n\\n**Mixed-effects model**, fit per benchmark and pooled:\\n\\n```\\nΔ_acc ~ condition + (1 | seed) + (1 | model_id)\\n```\\n\\nWhere *condition* is the 7-cell ablation grid (or the 3-cell subset for primary inference). Robust to imbalance. The contrast matrix is pre-registered against the §5 cells.\\n\\n### 8.2 Primary contrasts and α\\n- 3 primary contrasts, **α = 0.05 family-wise**, **Holm–Bonferroni** correction applied across the 3 primary contrasts.\\n- Reported with adjusted p-values, raw p-values available in supplementary appendix.\\n- Effect size: **Cohen's d** for paired across-seed differences (paired cell-by-seed within (model_id)); 95% CI by **bootstrap** (10,000 resamples at the seed level, stratified by model_id).\\n\\n### 8.3 Power and sample size\\n\\nTargets:\\n- ≥3 seeds; **target 5**.\\n- 2 model sizes (scaling probe) entered into the random-effects pool per Rubin's rules (§11).\\n\\nWith **5 seeds at one model size**, paired tests on benchmark deltas with a within-cell σ_Δ of ~5 percentage points (typical for BBH subset-scale deltas in small LMs) achieve **80% power to detect d ≈ 0.55** (i.e., ~2.75 pp absolute difference). With **3 seeds**, the analogous detectable d is ~0.85. The design therefore favors moderate-to-large effects; smaller effects may go undetected and are reported as such (failure to reject ≠ evidence of equality).\\n\\nIf only **3 seeds** are feasible, do not run the scaling probe.\\n\\n### 8.4 Supporting contrasts (secondary)\\n- Within each game: Full vs. Impl-only delta (paired across seed × imitation-corpus-size if 2.5K arm is run). Tests whether self-play helps at all given imitation.\\n- Game-impl vs. Baseline (game effect given no self-play). Tests whether imitation on game content alone transfers.\\n- League-opponent Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (sensitivity arm).\\n- PPO Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (optimizer arm).\\n- 7B vs. 13B interaction on Taboo-full vs. Q20-full contrast (scaling probe).\\n- Held-out game performance (overfitting check).\\n- Mechanistic diagnostic (§9): correlation between self-play reward trajectory slope and downstream Δ, per cell.\\n\\nAll supporting contrasts are reported with unadjusted p-values and labelled as exploratory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Mechanistic Diagnostics\\n\\nRequired to distinguish \\\"adversarial pressure drives transfer\\\" from \\\"self-play drives transfer\\\":\\n\\n- **Self-play reward → downstream Δ correlation:** per cell, plot episode success rate (rolling window over the last 500 episodes) against the rolling checkpoint's downstream Δ; compute Spearman ρ between reward slope and downstream Δ slope. **Locked expectation if H1a is true:** ρ is positive for Taboo and small or null for Q20 and City.\\n- **Taboo-violation rate trajectory:** the canonical mechanistic trace. A SPAG-style pipeline that retains a non-trivial taboo-violation rate throughout self-play is consistent with sustained adversarial pressure; if the rate collapses to zero early, H1a is undermined.\\n- **Turns-to-success trajectory:** convergence speed.\\n- **Discriminative probe (optional, budget permitting):** zero-shot transfer to a held-out *adversarial* reasoning task (e.g., adversarial Cloze, give-away puzzles) — if Taboo generalizes specifically to adversarial reasoning but not general reasoning, the H1a interpretation is narrowed; reported only as exploratory.\\n\\nThese diagnostics are pre-registered as mandatory regardless of headline outcome.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions Block (every inferred SPAG detail enumerated)\\n\\n**No item below is a citation of SPAG paper text.** Each is a parameter *fixed by this design*, in absence of source text. The design is robust to reasonable variations in all of them; each could be replaced by a researcher-provided value and the rest of the protocol is unchanged.\\n\\n**SPAG operational specifics (inferred defaults, replaceable):**\\n- A1. Base student model: `Llama-3-8B-Instruct` (fallback `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`). Scaling probe: 13B-class.\\n- A2. Self-play optimiser: Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT). Alternative arm: PPO. Optional arm: league + replay-buffer.\\n- A3. Opponent-sampling: frozen self-snapshot every K=500 episodes (default); K swept over {100, 500, 2000}.\\n- A4. Episode cap: T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}.\\n- A5. Self-play reward: +1 win / 0 otherwise (sparse end-of-episode); auxiliary taboo-violation rate tracked but not reward-scored.\\n- A6. Imitation corpus size: N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 arm as data-efficiency ablation.\\n- A7. GPT-4 prompting template: CoT-style system prompt encouraging strategic reasoning; identical scaffolding across games with only game-specific content (target word + taboo list / hidden object / hidden city) varied.\\n- A8. Optimiser hyperparameters for Phase A: AdamW, cosine LR, peak LR ∈ {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5} swept jointly across all cells.\\n- A9. Self-play episode budget: N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes on the winner pool.\\n- A10. Taboo word pool size: ≥5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs.\\n- A11. Number of seeds: ≥3, target 5.\\n- A12. Identical prompt scaffolding across games within ±5% system-token budget.\\n\\n**Framing assumptions (locked by the design):**\\n- A13. 20-Question is non-adversarial / cooperative (single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target, no zero-sum incentive).\\n- A14. Guess-My-City is non-adversarial (single agent, no opponent utility).\\n- A15. Taboo is adversarial (asymmetric role pair, opposing incentives, taboo-list avoidance constraint).\\n- A16. \\\"Reasoning improvement\\\" is measured by macro-aggregated Δ across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM, all zero-shot.\\n\\n**Reasoning-benchmark assumptions:**\\n- A17. BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style evaluations are commensurate with it.\\n- A18. ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination with the game's content pools (verified by §11 prompt-leakage audit).\\n\\n**Compute assumptions:**\\n- A19. Compute is expressible in 8 × A100-80G node GPU-hours; no other accelerator is assumed.\\n- A20. Sustained training throughput ≈ 150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32 mixed) for 7–13B-parameter transformer training including backward.\\n\\n**Inferential assumptions:**\\n- A21. Per-cell Δ-noise σ_Δ ≤ 5 pp across seeds for the primary benchmarks (assumption from LLM-evaluation literature; if violated, increase seeds).\\n- A22. Sensitivity to detect d ≥ 0.55 (5 seeds) / d ≥ 0.85 (3 seeds) at 80% power, α = 0.05.\\n- A23. Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate for downstream inference; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is conceptually a random-effects meta-analysis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Threats to Validity and Prompt-Leakage Audit\\n\\n**Threats to internal validity.**\\n- T1. **Spurious adversarial signal.** Taboo's taboo list may be present in BBH prompts (e.g., commonsense inference about forbidden words); mitigated by A10 / A18 and the audit below.\\n- T2. **In-game overfitting.** RSFT can overfit to the held-out training corpus; mitigated by the held-out game split and by reporting reward-vs-Δ correlation.\\n- T3. **Prompt-template confound.** If Taboo's natural prompt is longer than Q20's or City's, length alone could affect imitation quality; mitigated by A12.\\n- T4. **Default-taboo-list overlap with benchmarks.** Specifically tested by the audit.\\n\\n**Threats to construct validity.**\\n- T5. **Operationalization of \\\"adversarial\\\".** This design's contrast assumes 20-Question is non-adversarial. If SPAG's source treats 20-Question as zero-sum, the published paper and this design's question-disambiguation differ, and a *secondary* arm (20-Question as zero-sum, asker rewarded for information gain and answerer penalised) is added as a sensitivity analysis (§5 reduced-mode does not include this; full-mode would add an 8th primary condition pending compute).\\n- T6. **Reward-shaping confounds.** The choice to keep taboo-violation only as a diagnostic rather than a reward term is a design default; an alternative arm interprets taboo-violation as a small negative reward (e.g., −0.1 per violation). Default is end-of-episode reward.\\n- T7. **Optimizer confound.** RSFT is the default; PPO and league arms exist to attribute any null result to optimizer choice if needed.\\n\\n**Threats to external validity.**\\n- T8. **Model-family generalization.** Results are conditioned on the chosen base model; replication at 13B is the principal scaling check. Generalization to other families (Qwen vs. Llama) is reported as the family effect in the mixed model if both are run.\\n\\n**Prompt-leakage audit (mandatory, performed before launching training):**\\n1. **Token overlap check.** For each cell, sample 1,000 trajectories and 1,000 GPT-4 imitation completions. Compute (a) exact substring match of any taboo word, Q20 target, or city against BBH / GSM8K / ARC / MMLU items, and (b) 8-gram exact match. Any exact-match hit on a benchmark item triggers a content-pool revision before training.\\n2. **Standardized contamination screen.** Run the post-imitation checkpoint zero-shot on a *subset* of each benchmark and on benchmarks' reverse-engineered dummy items; non-trivial differences in accuracy indicate content leakage and require content-pool revision.\\n3. **Train/test split discipline.** Game-content pools and benchmark items must be split by content not by surface form: any candidate (target word, taboo list, hidden object, city) whose surface form overlaps an evaluation item is moved to a \\\"discard\\\" set.\\n4. **Persona check.** GPT-4's system prompt explicitly forbids it from introducing any terms that would solve a benchmark item verbatim; the system prompt is locked and audited post-hoc on 200 sampled generations.\\n\\nThe audit must clear before any cell is trained; failures block launch and require a content-pool revision.\\n\\n**Rubin's rules (for pooling model sizes):**\\n- When the scaling probe is run (7B + 13B), the mixed-effects model includes `model_id` as a random effect (§8.1). Reported pooled effects use Rubin's standard combination: weighted point estimate = Σ_k w_k θ̂_k with w_k = 1/σ̂_k²; variance = Σ_k w_k² σ̂_k² (cluster-robust bootstrap over seeds respects the pairing). Pooled estimates are *secondary* to model-family-specific estimates; both are reported.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Pre-registration Checklist\\n\\nLocked and versioned before any execution. Each item below is a *yes/no* gate.\\n\\n- [ ] Hypothesis registration (H0/H1a/H1b/H0-imitation/H0-selfplay/H0-game), omnibus model, contrasts, α, and power statement.\\n- [ ] 7-cell ablation grid with cell-defining axes (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, T, optimiser).\\n- [ ] 3 primary contrasts (Taboo−Q20, Taboo−City, Q20−City) with Holm–Bonferroni correction.\\n- [ ] Base-model default and scaling probe fixed (with re-parameterization note).\\n- [ ] Self-play optimiser default (RSFT) and freeze-snapshot opponent policy fixed; alternative arms documented.\\n- [ ] Imitation corpus size default (10,000) and 2,500 arm fixed.\\n- [ ] Prompt-scaffolding match-check across games (A12).\\n- [ ] Compute envelope: 8 × A100-80G node, FLOP-matched (A19/A20), budget table for 7-cell vs. reduced 3-cell.\\n- [ ] Seed plan (≥3, target 5).\\n- [ ] Downstream evaluation suite: BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM; all zero-shot, locked prompts.\\n- [ ] Reporting convention: Δ from post-imitation checkpoint; per-benchmark + macro-averaged; absolute from base as supplementary.\\n- [ ] Mechanistic diagnostics: reward-vs-Δ correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory, turns-to-success trajectory.\\n- [ ] Prompt-leakage audit protocol (T1–T4 / §11 audit steps 1–4) with gate-before-train requirement.\\n- [ ] Assumptions block (A1–A23) signed off by researcher.\\n- [ ] Threats-to-validity subsection (T1–T8) signed off.\\n- [ ] Hold-out split of game content and benchmark content, versioned and immutable.\\n- [ ] Rubin's-rules pooling procedure documented for the multi-model-size secondary estimate.\\n- [ ] Decoding settings, prompt templates, and few-shot settings locked.\\n- [ ] Random seeds enumerated in advance for each cell × model.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Compute Envelope Coarse Budget Table (illustrative; 8 × A100-80G node)\\n\\nFLOP estimates use 6N per token for forward+backward (training) and 2N per token for forward only (rollouts), at sustained 150 TFLOPs/node.\\n\\n| Cell | Imitation FLOPs (≈) | Self-play FLOPs (≈) | Eval FLOPs (≈) | Total node-hours (8B) | Total node-hours (13B) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Taboo-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Taboo-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\\n| Q20-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Q20-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\\n| City-impl | 5.5e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.0 | ~1.7 |\\n| City-full | 5.5e17 | 4.0e18 | 1.6e13 | ~8.5 | ~14.0 |\\n| Baseline | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| **Full factorial, 5 seeds, 8B** | | | | **~165 node-hours** | |\\n| **Full factorial, 5 seeds, 13B** | | | | | **~275 node-hours** |\\n| **Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 8B** | | | | **~145 node-hours** | |\\n| **Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 13B** | | | | | **~235 node-hours** |\\n| **Stretch (full factorial + 2.5K arms + league arm), 5 seeds, 8B** | | | | **~250 node-hours** | |\\n\\n(Orders-of-magnitude. Trajectory token budgets used: Taboo ≈ 1.5K tokens, Q20 ≈ 2K, City ≈ 0.5K; self-play episode 2× that with both sides. Cell counts × seed counts are included in the totals; model-id sweeps multiply by 2.)\\n\\n**Recommendation if compute is tight:** run reduced (3 full-game cells) at one model size with 5 seeds (~145 node-hours). If even reduced mode is infeasible, drop to 3 seeds (~85 node-hours).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. Pipeline Summary (one-paragraph TL;DR for the design file)\\n\\nA base open LM (default 8B; scaling probe 13B) is SFT'd on 10,000 GPT-4-played trajectories per game (Taboo, 20-Question, Guess-My-City) using a shared prompt scaffold (imitation-only cells); the post-imitation checkpoint then enters RSFT self-play against a frozen self-snapshot opponent refreshed every K=500 episodes over 20,000 episodes (full cells). A 7-cell ablation grid (Taboo/Q20/City × Full/Impl-only plus a no-game Baseline) is trained and evaluated; primary contrasts are Taboo-full vs. Q20-full, Taboo-full vs. City-full, and Q20-full vs. City-full, tested at α = 0.05 family-wise with Holm–Bonferroni correction; effect sizes reported as paired Cohen's d with 10,000-seed bootstrap CIs; mechanistic diagnostics (reward-vs-Δ correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory) are pre-registered as mandatory. Compute is FLOP-matched across cells within ±2%; a prompt-leakage audit gates training; reporting is per-benchmark and macro-averaged Δ from the post-imitation checkpoint; pooled multi-model-size estimates follow Rubin's rules with model_id as a random effect. The directoriestate of `/workspace` is empty aside from `task.json`; no SPAG paper text is imported; all SPAG operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters and listed in §10.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. What is intentionally not in this design\\n- **No fabricated experimental outcome.** The design predicts expected effect size for power calculation but reports no empirical finding.\\n- **No claim of execution.** Outputs of GPT-4, model checkpoints, or downstream metric values are not asserted; the design only specifies how they would be obtained and tested.\\n- **No invocation of network or external APIs.** The bubblewrap runtime precludes both; the design is parameterizable on whatever is eventually runnable.\\n- **No citation of SPAG paper text not present in the supplied context.** Every operational fact is an inferred default in §10; replacing any of them with a sourced value is a one-parameter edit.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository evidence: (a) `/workspace` contains exactly one file `task.json` (verified via `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 3`); (b) `task.json` body, read via `inspect_repository`, gives the research question, the \\\"design-only\\\" context, and three researcher topic IDs `neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33` whose content is not present in the workspace (the researcher has confirmed the topics list is empty and the bodies cannot be supplied); (c) repository-wide `find / -iname \\\"*spag*\\\"` and `find / -iname \\\"*taboo*\\\"` returned no relevant hits — no SPAG/Taboo paper or code is on disk. Environment evidence (from task contract): bubblewrap runtime; `network: disabled`; `gpu_enabled: false`; `cpu_affinity_count: 24`; `software_environment: system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled`; `datasets: []`; `note: \\\"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\\\"` Researcher-side evidence (latest message): explicit approval of the approach with five priority resolutions and seven additional guardrails (a)–(f), each reflected in a corresponding section of the design. No SPAG paper text is asserted; every value labelled \\\"default\\\" is a design-fixed parameter, not a citation.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Assumption A23: Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is a random-effects meta-analysis. — Statistical-modeling assumption.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Assumption A22: With 5 seeds, detectable d ≈ 0.55 at 80% power, α = 0.05; with 3 seeds, detectable d ≈ 0.85. — Power-derivation assumption.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A21: Per-cell Δ-noise σ_Δ ≤ 5 percentage points across seeds for primary benchmarks. — Inference-assumption from LLM-evaluation literature.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A20: Sustained training throughput ≈ 150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32) for 7–13B-parameter transformer training including backward. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A19: Compute is expressible in 8 × A100-80G node GPU-hours. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A18: ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination from game content pools; verified by §11 audit. — Verified not asserted.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A17: BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style scores are commensurate. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A16: \\\"Reasoning improvement\\\" is measured by macro-aggregated Δ across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM, zero-shot. — Locked by primary-suite selection.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A15: Taboo is adversarial (asymmetric role pair, opposing incentives, taboo-avoidance constraint). — Locked framing choice.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A14: Guess-My-City is non-adversarial (single agent, no opponent utility). — Locked framing choice.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A13: 20-Question is fixed as non-adversarial / cooperative (single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target). — Locked framing choice per researcher.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A12: System-prompt token budget matched across games within ±5%. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A11: Number of seeds ≥3, target 5. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A10: Taboo word pool ≥5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs, disjoint from reasoning benchmarks. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A9: Self-play episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A8: Phase-A optimizer is AdamW with cosine LR; peak LR swept jointly across cells over {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A7: GPT-4 system prompt is CoT-style and uses identical scaffolding across games, varying only game-specific content. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A6: Imitation corpus size N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 ablation arm. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A5: Self-play reward is sparse +1 win / 0 otherwise; taboo-violation rate is a diagnostic, not a reward term. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A4: Episode cap T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A3: Opponent-sampling default is a frozen self-snapshot refreshed every K=500 episodes; K swept over {100, 500, 2000}. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A2: Self-play optimizer default is Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT); alternatives PPO and league+replay-buffer are arms. — Design parameter.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Assumption A1: Base student model is `Llama-3-8B-Instruct` (fallback `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`); scaling probe at a 13B-class checkpoint. — Design parameter, not source-anchored.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have any n-gram overlap with Taboo/Q20/City content pools after the prompt-leakage audit — to be verified before launch; the audit gates training.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Whether a 13B-class checkpoint fits in the eventual compute envelope alongside the 7–8B default — unknown; the scaling probe is optional.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Exact compute envelope available to the experimenter — unknown; the design expresses all sizing in FLOPs and 8×A100-80G node-hours and gives bare-minimum (~85 node-hours, 3 seeds, reduced) and stretch (~250 node-hours with 2.5K and league arms) budgets.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether SPAG reports seed-paired statistics (needed for paired Cohen's d) or only point estimates — supply-bound; the design assumes seed-level variance is recoverable.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"SPAG's reported per-benchmark numbers — supply-bound; the design reports both per-benchmark and macro-averaged deltas to align with whatever the original paper reports.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"SPAG's exact GPT-4 prompting template (system prompt wording, CoT policy, role formatting) — supply-bound; design uses identical scaffolding with game-specific content swaps to neutralize template confounds.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"SPAG's exact episode length / turn cap distribution — supply-bound; default T=20 with a sweep.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"SPAG's exact opponent-sampling scheme (PSRO league, replay buffer, ELO-curated, frozen-snapshot) — supply-bound; the default frozen-snapshot is mirrored and league added as an optional arm.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"SPAG's exact reward structure (sparse vs. dense, partial credit for taboo violations, dense vs. sparse in Q20) — supply-bound; the default sparse +1/0 is replaced by a one-parameter edit if the researcher supplies a value.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether SPAG's own published framing of 20-Question is adversarial or cooperative — supply-bound; if adversarial, the secondary sensitivity arm (Q20-as-zero-sum) is added at the researcher's discretion and the published contrast is reported with a framing discrepancy note.</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-385f44b5-8946-4282-8b9e-3c9813b9847f", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 15}
{"id": "20fa400e-631e-4046-9dca-83ad61b67249", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:50:56.558845", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a4923787-5e75-4839-aec9-427fd3b6c8b4", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-f4301a73-bb52-4877-a576-3c0fa2e39b46", "error": "Error validating tool 'end_researcher_turn': 2 validation errors for ReviewHandoffAction\nassumptions.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...rce-anchored.</item>\\n'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type\nuncertainties.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...repancy note.</item>\\n\"}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type. Parameters provided: ['stage', 'answer', 'evidence', 'assumptions', 'uncertainties']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "01ebf76d-f285-4e2d-bc03-c54ecb9cda7a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:52:31.989430", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "20fa400e-631e-4046-9dca-83ad61b67249", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-be57b3cd-942e-4a82-84c0-b24607c17898", "tool_call": {"id": "call-be57b3cd-942e-4a82-84c0-b24607c17898", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Deliver full final experiment design document\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Final Experiment Design — SPAG Adversarial vs Non-Adversarial Self-Play for Reasoning\\n\\n**Status:** Design-only. No execution is claimed, no model weights are produced, no network or model APIs are invoked. All operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters and labelled as assumptions; the design is robust to reasonable variations in those parameters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Operating frame: what \\\"non-adversarial\\\" means in this design\\n\\nPer locked design choice, **20-Question and Guess-My-City have no adversary and no zero-sum incentive**. Specifically:\\n\\n- **20-Question:** a single asymmetric role pair — the *asker* elicits and the *answerer* answers truthfully toward a common hidden target. The answerer is rewarded for helping the asker succeed; there is no opposing side penalizing information disclosure. Success = asker identifies the target within the turn budget.\\n- **Guess-My-City:** a single agent that narrows a hidden city through its own questions/guesses. No counter-player, no opponent utility.\\n- **Taboo (SPAG):** an asymmetric role pair with opposing incentives — the describer must convey a target word *without* uttering any item from a taboo list issued in the prompt, while a guesser attempts to name the target. The taboo-avoidance constraint creates a zero-sum pressure against surface forms.\\n\\n**Assumption (locked):** Under this framing, \\\"adversarial\\\" is the Taboo condition alone; the contrast Taboo vs. {Q20, City} is the SPAG-vs-non-adversarial contrast the question asks about. If SPAG's own framing of 20-Question is zero-sum, that fact is not in the supplied context and therefore does not change this design — it would simply mean the published paper's contrast and the question's named contrast use different operationalizations, which is itself a result-of-interest and is reported as a framing discrepancy in §11 (Threats to Validity).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Background and Question\\n\\n**Background.** SPAG's pipeline, as described in the supplied task context, has two stages: (i) **imitation learning** of GPT-4 on game trajectories, and (ii) **self-play** on game episodes, with the *Adversarial Taboo* game being the proposed source of \\\"adversarial pressure\\\". The hypothesis under test is that the adversarial structure of Taboo uniquely transfers to general reasoning. Non-adversarial self-play environments (information-seeking but non-zero-sum, namely 20-Question and Guess-My-City) allow the contribution of self-play per se versus adversarial pressure per se to be separated.\\n\\n**Question.** Does SPAG (imitation + adversarial Taboo self-play) yield larger downstream reasoning gains than imitation + non-adversarial self-play on 20-Question or Guess-My-City, holding all other variables fixed?\\n\\n**Why this matters.** A positive answer implicates *adversarial pressure* as the active ingredient rather than self-play generally, with implications for which game families are worth investing in for downstream reasoning. A null answer implicates self-play *per se* (or imitation alone) as the active ingredient.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Hypotheses\\n\\n- **H0 (omnibus null):** Under FLOP-matched training, downstream reasoning Δ-accuracy is the same across the three self-play regimes (Taboo-full, Q20-full, City-full).\\n- **H1a (primary, directional):** Taboo-full > Q20-full and Taboo-full > City-full on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\\n- **H1b (secondary, directional):** Q20-full > City-full.\\n- **H0-imitation:** Imitation-only checkpoints (Taboo-impl, Q20-impl, City-impl) do not differ across games on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\\n- **H0-selfplay:** Within each game, the Full vs. Impl-only delta is zero.\\n- **H0-game:** The no-game baseline is indistinguishable from any Impl-only condition.\\n\\nThe primary inference targets H1a; everything else is a supporting contrast.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Independent and Dependent Variables\\n\\n### Independent variable (single primary)\\n- **Self-play environment**, 7-cell ablation grid (§5). The 3-cell omnibus contrast (full only) is the primary subset.\\n\\n### Dependent variables (locked)\\n\\n**Downstream reasoning (primary, locked-prompt, zero-shot):**\\n- **BBH** (BIG-Bench Hard) — headline number; macro-averaged over the 23 subtasks.\\n- **GSM8K** — multi-step arithmetic reasoning; reports strict-match accuracy.\\n- **ARC-Challenge** — general scientific reasoning; reports accuracy.\\n- **MMLU-STEM** — secondary general check (physics, chemistry, biology, math, CS, engineering); reports accuracy.\\n\\nReport per-benchmark and **macro-averaged** deltas (mean of the four per-benchmark normalized deltas).\\n\\n**In-game diagnostics (mechanistic, secondary):**\\n- Episode success rate (per game).\\n- Mean turns-to-success.\\n- Taboo-violation rate (fraction of describer episodes in which ≥1 taboo word was uttered) — diagnostic of whether Taboo produces the intended adversarial pressure.\\n- Self-play reward trajectory across self-play steps.\\n\\n**Reporting convention:** All downstream results are reported as Δ from the **post-imitation** checkpoint of the corresponding game. This isolates the self-play contribution from the imitation contribution. An \\\"absolute\\\" report (vs. base model) is included alongside for each condition because the literature often expects both.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Pipeline (Imitation + Self-Play)\\n\\n### 4.1 Phase A — Imitation\\n- **Teacher:** GPT-4 (locked).\\n- **Default corpus:** N_imitation = **10,000 trajectories per game**, with a smaller **N=2,500** arm as a data-efficiency ablation (§5).\\n- **Generation mechanism (assumed default):** GPT-4 plays both sides of each episode (or both roles for Q20/City) under a system prompt encouraging CoT-style strategic reasoning; the student's role transcript is the training target. **Cross-game prompt template reuse** — same scaffolding (system instructions, turn markers), with only the game-specific content (target word + taboo list / hidden object / hidden city) varied. This is the principal control for prompt-format confounds.\\n- **Student optimisation (assumed default):** standard SFT / next-token cross-entropy; 2–3 epochs over the corpus; AdamW; cosine LR schedule; peak LR ∈ {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5} swept (single shared sweep across cells).\\n- **Output:** one post-imitation checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, model_id, seed).\\n\\n### 4.2 Phase B — Self-Play\\n- **Default optimiser:** **Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT)** — keep winning trajectories, fine-tune on them with cross-entropy. Frozen-snapshot opponent.\\n- **Opponent-sampling default:** frozen copy of the student, snapshotted every **K episodes** (default **K=500**; sweep over K ∈ {100, 500, 2000} as a sensitivity probe).\\n- **Optional arm:** **League / replay-buffer** opponent pool (ensemble of past snapshots with ELO-curated sampling) — mirrors the protocol-level family of designs that SPAG-style papers sometimes use.\\n- **Alternative arm (not default):** PPO with the same frozen-snapshot opponent and a learned value head; included only to verify the optimizer choice is not the bottleneck for the H1a conclusion.\\n- **Reward signals (assumed defaults):**\\n  - Taboo: +1 if guesser names the target before turn cap; 0 otherwise. Taboo-word violations are tracked as an auxiliary diagnostic metric, not as a reward term (i.e., the reward is end-of-episode outcome).\\n  - 20-Question: +1 if asker names the hidden target within turn cap; partial credit ±0.25 per quartile of information gain is allowed but is excluded from the default reward and reported only as a sensitivity arm.\\n  - Guess-My-City: +1 if the agent names the hidden city within turn cap.\\n- **Episode cap:** T = 20 turns per game (default); sweep T ∈ {10, 20, 40} as a sensitivity arm.\\n- **Episode budget per cell:** N_selfplay = **20,000 episodes** (default); RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes on the rolling winner pool (10 SFT passes per cell, locked across cells).\\n- **Output:** one post-self-play checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, seed, model_id).\\n\\n### 4.3 Evaluation phase\\n- Every checkpoint is evaluated on (a) the locked downstream reasoning suite and (b) a held-out *game* split (5,000 episodes per game, distinct content from any training/trajectory content) to monitor in-game overfitting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. 7-Cell Ablation Grid\\n\\nThe factorial below is the pre-registered primary design. Cells are matched on FLOPs, prompts, optimiser (RSFT default), base model, and seeds.\\n\\n| # | Cell | Imitation | Self-play | Game | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Taboo-impl | yes | — | Taboo | isolates imitation contribution |\\n| 2 | **Taboo-full (SPAG)** | yes | yes | Taboo | **primary condition** |\\n| 3 | Q20-impl | yes | — | 20-Question | |\\n| 4 | Q20-full | yes | yes | 20-Question | primary non-adversarial #1 |\\n| 5 | City-impl | yes | — | Guess-My-City | |\\n| 6 | City-full | yes | yes | Guess-My-City | primary non-adversarial #2 |\\n| 7 | Baseline | — | — | — | generic instruct-only FT (no game); same SFT recipe, same FLOPs, same data volume |\\n\\n**Reduced (bare-minimum) mode:** cells {2, 4, 6} only — three full-game cells, no ablation cells. Used if budget cannot stretch to the full factorial.\\n\\n**Stretch (if compute permits):** add an 8th and 9th cell using the **N=2,500 imitation arm** at the post-self-play step (Taboo-full-2.5K, Q20-full-2.5K, City-full-2.5K) to probe data efficiency; and a 10th cell using the **League opponent** arm at Taboo-full.\\n\\n**Pre-registered primary contrasts (3):**\\n1. Taboo-full vs. Q20-full (Taboo − Q20 on macro-averaged Δ).\\n2. Taboo-full vs. City-full (Taboo − City).\\n3. Q20-full vs. City-full (Q20 − City).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Controls and Matching\\n\\nThe match-the-stuff list. Every item below is fixed identically across all cells unless explicitly varied as a cell-defining axis.\\n\\n- **Base model:** family-agnostic; default `Llama-3-8B-Instruct` (or an equivalent-size `Qwen2.5-7B-(-Instruct)` if Llama-3 unavailable). All cells use the same base checkpoint. Scaling probe: re-run **Taboo-full, Q20-full, City-full** at a 13B-class checkpoint (e.g., `Llama-3-13B-Instruct` or `Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct`) under the same protocol, treated as a model_id random effect.\\n- **Compute:** FLOP-matched. Each cell's total training FLOPs (Phase A + Phase B) is constrained to be within ±2% across cells. Concretely: if Taboo requires more tokens per self-play episode due to taboo-list conditioning, either (a) extend Q20/City episodes to the same token budget or (b) cap Taboo self-play episodes so its total tokens match. Default: option (a) — equalize per-episode token budget; report sensitivity under option (b).\\n- **Imitation data:** N_imitation matched across game cells (10,000 default).\\n- **Self-play episodes:** matched (20,000 default) and matched per-cell FLOPs on top.\\n- **Optimizer hyperparameters:** AdamW, cosine LR, identical peak LR (swept once jointly, not per cell); identical warm-up; identical batch size; identical gradient clipping; identical precision (bf16/fp32 mixed).\\n- **Prompts:** identical scaffolding; game-specific content swapped. The system-prompt token budget is matched to within ±5%.\\n- **Decoding during evaluation:** temperature 0, max-new-tokens cap, identical for all cells.\\n- **Few-shot settings:** zero-shot on all four downstream suites; locked.\\n- **Seeds:** ≥3 seeds, target **5** seeds, identically seeded across cells (paired seed design).\\n- **Game-content pools:**\\n  - Taboo word pool: ≥5,000 distinct (target, taboo-list) pairs, disjoint from any reasoning-benchmark text.\\n  - Q20 object pool: ≥5,000 distinct common-noun targets, disjoint from any reasoning-benchmark text.\\n  - City pool: ≥5,000 cities not appearing in any reasoning-benchmark text or in BBH subtask instances.\\n  - See §11 (Prompt-leakage audit).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Evaluation Suite\\n\\nLocked prompt suite for downstream evaluation. Every cell is evaluated under identical prompts, with the prompt templates and decoding settings versioned and stored alongside the design document.\\n\\n- **BBH:** all 23 subtasks; the canonical BBH evaluation prompt for each subtask; aggregate via unweighted macro-average across subtasks. Report per-subtask score in the supplementary appendix.\\n- **GSM8K:** canonical 8-shot prompt (locked) **OR** zero-shot CoT with locked prompt — the design fixes zero-shot and locks the CoT template; the 8-shot version is reported as a sensitivity number if pre-registered capacity allows.\\n- **ARC-Challenge:** test split; zero-shot; canonical prompt.\\n- **MMLU-STEM:** zero-shot; subject-level scores aggregated to MMLU-STEM by unweighted macro-average.\\n\\n**Reporting metric:** Δ-accuracy = (post-self-play score) − (post-imitation score) for the same (game, seed). Macro-averaged Δ = mean of the four per-benchmark Δs.\\n\\n**Held-out game split:** 5,000 episodes per game, content disjoint from training/trajectory content; evaluated for episode success rate, mean turns-to-success, and (Taboo) taboo-violation rate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Statistical Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 8.1 Pre-registered model\\n\\n**Mixed-effects model**, fit per benchmark and pooled:\\n\\n```\\nΔ_acc ~ condition + (1 | seed) + (1 | model_id)\\n```\\n\\nWhere *condition* is the 7-cell ablation grid (or the 3-cell subset for primary inference). Robust to imbalance. The contrast matrix is pre-registered against the §5 cells.\\n\\n### 8.2 Primary contrasts and α\\n- 3 primary contrasts, **α = 0.05 family-wise**, **Holm–Bonferroni** correction applied across the 3 primary contrasts.\\n- Reported with adjusted p-values, raw p-values available in supplementary appendix.\\n- Effect size: **Cohen's d** for paired across-seed differences (paired cell-by-seed within (model_id)); 95% CI by **bootstrap** (10,000 resamples at the seed level, stratified by model_id).\\n\\n### 8.3 Power and sample size\\n\\nTargets:\\n- ≥3 seeds, **target 5 seeds**.\\n- 2 model sizes (scaling probe) entered into the random-effects pool per Rubin's rules (§11).\\n\\nWith **5 seeds at one model size**, paired tests on benchmark deltas with a within-cell σ_Δ of ~5 percentage points (typical for BBH subset-scale deltas in small LMs) achieve **80% power to detect d ≈ 0.55** (i.e., ~2.75 pp absolute difference). With **3 seeds**, the analogous detectable d is ~0.85. The design therefore favors moderate-to-large effects; smaller effects may go undetected and are reported as such (failure to reject does not equal evidence of equality).\\n\\nIf only **3 seeds** are feasible, do not run the scaling probe.\\n\\n### 8.4 Supporting contrasts (secondary)\\n- Within each game: Full vs. Impl-only delta (paired across seed × imitation-corpus-size if 2.5K arm is run). Tests whether self-play helps at all given imitation.\\n- Game-impl vs. Baseline (game effect given no self-play). Tests whether imitation on game content alone transfers.\\n- League-opponent Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (sensitivity arm).\\n- PPO Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (optimizer arm).\\n- 7B vs. 13B interaction on Taboo-full vs. Q20-full contrast (scaling probe).\\n- Held-out game performance (overfitting check).\\n- Mechanistic diagnostic (§9): correlation between self-play reward trajectory slope and downstream Δ, per cell.\\n\\nAll supporting contrasts are reported with unadjusted p-values and labelled as exploratory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Mechanistic Diagnostics\\n\\nRequired to distinguish \\\"adversarial pressure drives transfer\\\" from \\\"self-play drives transfer\\\":\\n\\n- **Self-play reward → downstream Δ correlation:** per cell, plot episode success rate (rolling window over the last 500 episodes) against the rolling checkpoint's downstream Δ; compute Spearman ρ between reward slope and downstream Δ slope. **Locked expectation if H1a is true:** ρ is positive for Taboo and small or null for Q20 and City.\\n- **Taboo-violation rate trajectory:** the canonical mechanistic trace. A SPAG-style pipeline that retains a non-trivial taboo-violation rate throughout self-play is consistent with sustained adversarial pressure; if the rate collapses to zero early, H1a is undermined.\\n- **Turns-to-success trajectory:** convergence speed.\\n- **Discriminative probe (optional, budget permitting):** zero-shot transfer to a held-out *adversarial* reasoning task (e.g., adversarial Cloze, give-away puzzles) — if Taboo generalizes specifically to adversarial reasoning but not general reasoning, the H1a interpretation is narrowed; reported only as exploratory.\\n\\nThese diagnostics are pre-registered as mandatory regardless of headline outcome.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions Block (every inferred SPAG detail enumerated)\\n\\n**No item below is a citation of SPAG paper text.** Each is a parameter *fixed by this design*, in absence of source text. The design is robust to reasonable variations in all of them; each could be replaced by a researcher-provided value and the rest of the protocol is unchanged.\\n\\n**SPAG operational specifics (inferred defaults, replaceable):**\\n- A1. Base student model: `Llama-3-8B-Instruct` (fallback `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`). Scaling probe: 13B-class.\\n- A2. Self-play optimiser: Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT). Alternative arm: PPO. Optional arm: league + replay-buffer.\\n- A3. Opponent-sampling: frozen self-snapshot every K=500 episodes (default); K swept over {100, 500, 2000}.\\n- A4. Episode cap: T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}.\\n- A5. Self-play reward: +1 win / 0 otherwise (sparse end-of-episode); auxiliary taboo-violation rate tracked but not reward-scored.\\n- A6. Imitation corpus size: N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 arm as data-efficiency ablation.\\n- A7. GPT-4 prompting template: CoT-style system prompt encouraging strategic reasoning; identical scaffolding across games with only game-specific content (target word + taboo list / hidden object / hidden city) varied.\\n- A8. Optimiser hyperparameters for Phase A: AdamW, cosine LR, peak LR ∈ {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5} swept jointly across all cells.\\n- A9. Self-play episode budget: N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes on the winner pool.\\n- A10. Taboo word pool size: ≥5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs.\\n- A11. Number of seeds: ≥3, target 5.\\n- A12. Identical prompt scaffolding across games within ±5% system-token budget.\\n\\n**Framing assumptions (locked by the design):**\\n- A13. 20-Question is non-adversarial / cooperative (single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target, no zero-sum incentive).\\n- A14. Guess-My-City is non-adversarial (single agent, no opponent utility).\\n- A15. Taboo is adversarial (asymmetric role pair, opposing incentives, taboo-list avoidance constraint).\\n- A16. \\\"Reasoning improvement\\\" is measured by macro-aggregated Δ across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM, all zero-shot.\\n\\n**Reasoning-benchmark assumptions:**\\n- A17. BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style evaluations are commensurate with it.\\n- A18. ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination with the game's content pools (verified by §11 prompt-leakage audit).\\n\\n**Compute assumptions:**\\n- A19. Compute is expressible in 8 × A100-80G node GPU-hours; no other accelerator is assumed.\\n- A20. Sustained training throughput ≈ 150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32 mixed) for 7–13B-parameter transformer training including backward.\\n\\n**Inferential assumptions:**\\n- A21. Per-cell Δ-noise σ_Δ ≤ 5 pp across seeds for the primary benchmarks (assumption from LLM-evaluation literature; if violated, increase seeds).\\n- A22. Sensitivity to detect d ≥ 0.55 (5 seeds) / d ≥ 0.85 (3 seeds) at 80% power, α = 0.05.\\n- A23. Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate for downstream inference; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is conceptually a random-effects meta-analysis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Threats to Validity and Prompt-Leakage Audit\\n\\n**Threats to internal validity.**\\n- T1. **Spurious adversarial signal.** Taboo's taboo list may be present in BBH prompts (e.g., commonsense inference about forbidden words); mitigated by A10 / A18 and the audit below.\\n- T2. **In-game overfitting.** RSFT can overfit to the held-out training corpus; mitigated by the held-out game split and by reporting reward-vs-Δ correlation.\\n- T3. **Prompt-template confound.** If Taboo's natural prompt is longer than Q20's or City's, length alone could affect imitation quality; mitigated by A12.\\n- T4. **Default-taboo-list overlap with benchmarks.** Specifically tested by the audit.\\n\\n**Threats to construct validity.**\\n- T5. **Operationalization of \\\"adversarial\\\".** This design's contrast assumes 20-Question is non-adversarial. If SPAG's source treats 20-Question as zero-sum, the published paper and this design's question-disambiguation differ, and a *secondary* arm (20-Question as zero-sum, asker rewarded for information gain and answerer penalised) is added as a sensitivity analysis (§5 reduced-mode does not include this; full-mode would add an 8th primary condition pending compute).\\n- T6. **Reward-shaping confounds.** The choice to keep taboo-violation only as a diagnostic rather than a reward term is a design default; an alternative arm interprets taboo-violation as a small negative reward (e.g., −0.1 per violation). Default is end-of-episode reward.\\n- T7. **Optimizer confound.** RSFT is the default; PPO and league arms exist to attribute any null result to optimizer choice if needed.\\n\\n**Threats to external validity.**\\n- T8. **Model-family generalization.** Results are conditioned on the chosen base model; replication at 13B is the principal scaling check. Generalization to other families (Qwen vs. Llama) is reported as the family effect in the mixed model if both are run.\\n\\n**Prompt-leakage audit (mandatory, performed before launching training):**\\n1. **Token overlap check.** For each cell, sample 1,000 trajectories and 1,000 GPT-4 imitation completions. Compute (a) exact substring match of any taboo word, Q20 target, or city against BBH / GSM8K / ARC / MMLU items, and (b) 8-gram exact match. Any exact-match hit on a benchmark item triggers a content-pool revision before training.\\n2. **Standardized contamination screen.** Run the post-imitation checkpoint zero-shot on a *subset* of each benchmark and on benchmarks' reverse-engineered dummy items; non-trivial differences in accuracy indicate content leakage and require content-pool revision.\\n3. **Train/test split discipline.** Game-content pools and benchmark items must be split by content not by surface form: any candidate (target word, taboo list, hidden object, city) whose surface form overlaps an evaluation item is moved to a \\\"discard\\\" set.\\n4. **Persona check.** GPT-4's system prompt explicitly forbids it from introducing any terms that would solve a benchmark item verbatim; the system prompt is locked and audited post-hoc on 200 sampled generations.\\n\\nThe audit must clear before any cell is trained; failures block launch and require a content-pool revision.\\n\\n**Rubin's rules (for pooling model sizes):**\\n- When the scaling probe is run (7B + 13B), the mixed-effects model includes `model_id` as a random effect (§8.1). Reported pooled effects use Rubin's standard combination: weighted point estimate = Σ_k w_k θ̂_k with w_k = 1/σ̂_k²; variance = Σ_k w_k² σ̂_k² (cluster-robust bootstrap over seeds respects the pairing). Pooled estimates are *secondary* to model-family-specific estimates; both are reported.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Pre-registration Checklist\\n\\nLocked and versioned before any execution. Each item below is a *yes/no* gate.\\n\\n- [ ] Hypothesis registration (H0/H1a/H1b/H0-imitation/H0-selfplay/H0-game), omnibus model, contrasts, α, and power statement.\\n- [ ] 7-cell ablation grid with cell-defining axes (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, T, optimiser).\\n- [ ] 3 primary contrasts (Taboo−Q20, Taboo−City, Q20−City) with Holm–Bonferroni correction.\\n- [ ] Base-model default and scaling probe fixed (with re-parameterization note).\\n- [ ] Self-play optimiser default (RSFT) and freeze-snapshot opponent policy fixed; alternative arms documented.\\n- [ ] Imitation corpus size default (10,000) and 2,500 arm fixed.\\n- [ ] Prompt-scaffolding match-check across games (A12).\\n- [ ] Compute envelope: 8 × A100-80G node, FLOP-matched (A19/A20), budget table for 7-cell vs. reduced 3-cell.\\n- [ ] Seed plan (≥3, target 5).\\n- [ ] Downstream evaluation suite: BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM; all zero-shot, locked prompts.\\n- [ ] Reporting convention: Δ from post-imitation checkpoint; per-benchmark + macro-averaged; absolute from base as supplementary.\\n- [ ] Mechanistic diagnostics: reward-vs-Δ correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory, turns-to-success trajectory.\\n- [ ] Prompt-leakage audit protocol (T1–T4 / §11 audit steps 1–4) with gate-before-train requirement.\\n- [ ] Assumptions block (A1–A23) signed off by researcher.\\n- [ ] Threats-to-validity subsection (T1–T8) signed off.\\n- [ ] Hold-out split of game content and benchmark content, versioned and immutable.\\n- [ ] Rubin's-rules pooling procedure documented for the multi-model-size secondary estimate.\\n- [ ] Decoding settings, prompt templates, and few-shot settings locked.\\n- [ ] Random seeds enumerated in advance for each cell × model.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Compute Envelope Coarse Budget Table (illustrative; 8 × A100-80G node)\\n\\nFLOP estimates use 6N per token for forward+backward (training) and 2N per token for forward only (rollouts), at sustained 150 TFLOPs/node.\\n\\n| Cell | Imitation FLOPs (≈) | Self-play FLOPs (≈) | Eval FLOPs (≈) | Total node-hours (8B) | Total node-hours (13B) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Taboo-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Taboo-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\\n| Q20-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Q20-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\\n| City-impl | 5.5e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.0 | ~1.7 |\\n| City-full | 5.5e17 | 4.0e18 | 1.6e13 | ~8.5 | ~14.0 |\\n| Baseline | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Full factorial, 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~165 node-hours | |\\n| Full factorial, 5 seeds, 13B | | | | | ~275 node-hours |\\n| Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~145 node-hours | |\\n| Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 13B | | | | | ~235 node-hours |\\n| Stretch (full factorial + 2.5K arms + league arm), 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~250 node-hours | |\\n\\n(Orders-of-magnitude. Trajectory token budgets used: Taboo ≈ 1.5K tokens, Q20 ≈ 2K, City ≈ 0.5K; self-play episode 2× that with both sides. Cell counts × seed counts are included in the totals; model-id sweeps multiply by 2.)\\n\\n**Recommendation if compute is tight:** run reduced (3 full-game cells) at one model size with 5 seeds (~145 node-hours). If even reduced mode is infeasible, drop to 3 seeds (~85 node-hours).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. Pipeline Summary (one-paragraph TL;DR for the design file)\\n\\nA base open LM (default 8B; scaling probe 13B) is SFT'd on 10,000 GPT-4-played trajectories per game (Taboo, 20-Question, Guess-My-City) using a shared prompt scaffold (imitation-only cells); the post-imitation checkpoint then enters RSFT self-play against a frozen self-snapshot opponent refreshed every K=500 episodes over 20,000 episodes (full cells). A 7-cell ablation grid (Taboo/Q20/City × Full/Impl-only plus a no-game Baseline) is trained and evaluated; primary contrasts are Taboo-full vs. Q20-full, Taboo-full vs. City-full, and Q20-full vs. City-full, tested at α = 0.05 family-wise with Holm–Bonferroni correction; effect sizes reported as paired Cohen's d with 10,000-seed bootstrap CIs; mechanistic diagnostics (reward-vs-Δ correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory) are pre-registered as mandatory. Compute is FLOP-matched across cells within ±2%; a prompt-leakage audit gates training; reporting is per-benchmark and macro-averaged Δ from the post-imitation checkpoint; pooled multi-model-size estimates follow Rubin's rules with model_id as a random effect. The directory state of `/workspace` is empty aside from `task.json`; no SPAG paper text is imported; all SPAG operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters and listed in §10.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. What is intentionally not in this design\\n- **No fabricated experimental outcome.** The design predicts expected effect size for power calculation but reports no empirical finding.\\n- **No claim of execution.** Outputs of GPT-4, model checkpoints, or downstream metric values are not asserted; the design only specifies how they would be obtained and tested.\\n- **No invocation of network or external APIs.** The bubblewrap runtime precludes both; the design is parameterizable on whatever is eventually runnable.\\n- **No citation of SPAG paper text not present in the supplied context.** Every operational fact is an inferred default in §10; replacing any of them with a sourced value is a one-parameter edit.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository evidence: (a) `/workspace` contains exactly one file `task.json` (verified via `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 3`); (b) `task.json` body, read via `inspect_repository`, contains the research question, the \\\"design-only\\\" context line, and three researcher topic IDs `neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2`, `setup_32`, `setup_33` whose content is not present in the workspace (the researcher has confirmed the topics list is empty and the bodies cannot be supplied); (c) repository-wide `find / -iname \\\"*spag*\\\"` and `find / -iname \\\"*taboo*\\\"` returned no relevant hits — no SPAG/Taboo paper or code is on disk. Environment evidence (from task contract): bubblewrap runtime; `network: disabled`; `gpu_enabled: false`; `cpu_affinity_count: 24`; `software_environment: system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled`; `datasets: []`; `note: \\\"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\\\"` Researcher-side evidence (latest message): explicit approval of the approach with five priority resolutions (20-Question as non-adversarial cooperative; model-family-agnostic with 8B default and 13B scaling probe; BBH/GSM8K/ARC/MMLU-STEM as primary suite; RSFT as default with PPO/league as arms; 10K imitation default with 2.5K ablation; compute in 8xA100-80G node-hours) and seven guardrails (cooperative-framing explicitness, prompt-leakage audit, mechanistic diagnostics, pre-registered mixed-effects with Holm-Bonferroni over three primary contrasts and Cohen's d with bootstrap CIs, feasibility and threat-to-validity with Rubin pooling, fifteen-section documents structure, stop-without-execution). The design reflects every guardrail in a corresponding numbered section. No SPAG paper text is asserted; every value labelled \\\"default\\\" or \\\"sweep\\\" is a design-fixed parameter, not a citation.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"A23. Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is a random-effects meta-analysis. — Statistical-modeling assumption.</item>\",\"$text\":\"A22. With 5 seeds, detectable d ≈ 0.55 at 80% power, α = 0.05; with 3 seeds, detectable d ≈ 0.85. — Power-derivation assumption.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A21. Per-cell ∆-noise σ_∆ ≤ 5 percentage points across seeds for primary benchmarks. — Inference assumption from LLM-evaluation literature.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A20. Sustained training throughput ≈ 150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32) for 7–13B-parameter transformer training including backward. — Design parameter for the coarse budget table.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A19. Compute is expressible in 8 × A100-80G node GPU-hours. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A18. ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination with game content pools; verified by §11 audit (which gates training). — Verified not asserted.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A17. BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style scores are commensurate. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A16. \\\"Reasoning improvement\\\" is measured by macro-aggregated ∆ across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM, zero-shot. — Locked by primary-suite selection.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A15. Taboo is adversarial (asymmetric role pair, opposing incentives, taboo-avoidance constraint). — Locked framing choice.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A14. Guess-My-City is non-adversarial (single agent, no opponent utility). — Locked framing choice.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A13. 20-Question is fixed as non-adversarial / cooperative (single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target). — Locked framing choice per researcher.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A12. System-prompt token budget matched across games within ±5%. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A11. Number of seeds ≥3, target 5. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A10. Taboo word pool ≥5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs, disjoint from reasoning benchmarks. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A9. Self-play episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A8. Phase-A optimizer is AdamW with cosine LR; peak LR swept jointly across cells over {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A7. GPT-4 system prompt is CoT-style and uses identical scaffolding across games, varying only game-specific content. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A6. Imitation corpus size N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 ablation arm. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A5. Self-play reward is sparse +1 win / 0 otherwise; taboo-violation rate is a diagnostic, not a reward term. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A4. Episode cap T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A3. Opponent-sampling default is a frozen self-snapshot refreshed every K=500 episodes; K swept over {100, 500, 2000}. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A2. Self-play optimizer default is Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT); alternatives PPO and league+replay-buffer are arms. — Design parameter.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"A1. Base student model is `Llama-3-8B-Instruct` (fallback `Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct`); scaling probe at a 13B-class checkpoint. — Design parameter, not source-anchored.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether SPAG's own published framing of 20-Question is adversarial or cooperative — supply-bound; if adversarial, an additional sensitivity arm (Q20-as-zero-sum) is added at the researcher's discretion and the published contrast is reported with a framing discrepancy note in §11.</item>\",\"$text\":\"SPAG's exact reward structure (sparse vs. dense, partial credit for taboo violations, dense vs. sparse in Q20) — supply-bound; the default sparse +1/0 is replaced by a one-parameter edit if the researcher supplies a value.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"SPAG's exact opponent-sampling scheme (PSRO league