{"id": "7a78abb4-07de-4ccb-86b0-b6c2530ef262", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:52:31.989946", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "01ebf76d-f285-4e2d-bc03-c54ecb9cda7a", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-be57b3cd-942e-4a82-84c0-b24607c17898", "error": "Error validating tool 'end_researcher_turn': 2 validation errors for ReviewHandoffAction\nassumptions.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...ource-anchored.</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type\nuncertainties.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...gates training.</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type. Parameters provided: ['stage', 'answer', 'evidence', 'assumptions', 'uncertainties']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
{"id": "66c51c2c-d773-4759-ad8e-f1b1ac9fcbcf", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:53:47.845891", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7a78abb4-07de-4ccb-86b0-b6c2530ef262", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Experiment Design — SPAG Adversarial vs Non-Adversarial Self-Play for Reasoning\n\n**Status:** Design-only. No execution is claimed, no model weights are produced, no network or model APIs are invoked. All operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters and labelled as assumptions; the design is robust to reasonable variations in those parameters.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Operating frame: what \"non-adversarial\" means in this design\n\nPer locked design choice, **20-Question and Guess-My-City have no adversary and no zero-sum incentive**. Specifically:\n\n- 20-Question: a single asymmetric role pair — the asker elicits and the answerer answers truthfully toward a common hidden target. The answerer is rewarded for helping the asker succeed; there is no opposing side penalising information disclosure. Success = asker identifies the target within the turn budget.\n- Guess-My-City: a single agent that narrows a hidden city through its own questions/guesses. No counter-player, no opponent utility.\n- Taboo (SPAG): an asymmetric role pair with opposing incentives — the describer must convey a target word without uttering any item from a taboo list, while a guesser attempts to name the target.\n\n**Assumption (locked):** \"Adversarial\" = Taboo alone; the contrast is Taboo vs. {Q20, City}. If SPAG's source treats 20-Question as zero-sum, that fact is not in the supplied context; this would be reported as a framing discrepancy in §11.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Background and Question\n\n**Background.** SPAG's pipeline, as described in the supplied task context, has two stages: (i) imitation learning of GPT-4 on game trajectories, and (ii) self-play on game episodes, with the Adversarial Taboo game as the source of \"adversarial pressure\".\n\n**Question.** Does SPAG (imitation + adversarial Taboo self-play) yield larger downstream reasoning gains than imitation + non-adversarial self-play on 20-Question or Guess-My-City, holding all other variables fixed?\n\n**Why this matters.** A positive answer implicates adversarial pressure as the active ingredient rather than self-play generally. A null answer implicates self-play *per se* (or imitation alone) as the active ingredient.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Hypotheses\n\n- H0 (omnibus null): Under FLOP-matched training, downstream reasoning Δ-accuracy is the same across the three self-play regimes (Taboo-full, Q20-full, City-full).\n- H1a (primary, directional): Taboo-full > Q20-full and Taboo-full > City-full on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\n- H1b (secondary, directional): Q20-full > City-full.\n- H0-imitation: Imitation-only checkpoints (Taboo-impl, Q20-impl, City-impl) do not differ across games on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\n- H0-selfplay: Within each game, the Full vs. Impl-only delta is zero.\n- H0-game: The no-game baseline is indistinguishable from any Impl-only condition.\n\nPrimary inference targets H1a; everything else is supporting.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Independent and Dependent Variables\n\n**Independent variable.** Self-play environment, 7-cell ablation grid; primary subset is the 3-cell full-only contrast.\n\n**Downstream reasoning (primary, locked-prompt, zero-shot):**\n- BBH (BIG-Bench Hard) — headline; macro-averaged across 23 subtasks.\n- GSM8K — multi-step arithmetic; strict-match accuracy.\n- ARC-Challenge — scientific reasoning; accuracy.\n- MMLU-STEM — secondary general check; accuracy.\n\nReporting: per-benchmark and macro-averaged Δ (mean of four normalised per-benchmark Δs).\n\n**In-game diagnostics (mechanistic, secondary):**\n- Episode success rate (per game); mean turns-to-success; (Taboo) taboo-violation rate; self-play reward trajectory.\n\nReporting convention: downstream reported as Δ from the **post-imitation** checkpoint of the corresponding game. Absolute scores (vs. base model) reported alongside.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Pipeline (Imitation + Self-Play)\n\n### 4.1 Phase A — Imitation\n- Teacher: GPT-4.\n- Default N_imitation = 10,000 trajectories per game, with a 2,500-trajectory ablation arm (§5).\n- GPT-4 plays both sides under a CoT-style system prompt; the student's role transcript is the training target. Cross-game prompt template reuse — same scaffolding (system instructions, turn markers), game-specific content swapped. This is the principal control for prompt-format confounds.\n- Student optimisation: SFT / next-token cross-entropy; 2–3 epochs; AdamW; cosine LR schedule; peak LR ∈ {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5} swept jointly across cells.\n- Output: one post-imitation checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, model_id, seed).\n\n### 4.2 Phase B — Self-Play\n- Default optimiser: Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT) — keep winning trajectories, fine-tune on them; frozen-snapshot opponent.\n- Opponent-sampling default: frozen copy of the student, snapshotted every K episodes (default K=500; sweep over {100, 500, 2000}).\n- Optional arm: League / replay-buffer opponent pool (ensemble of past snapshots with ELO-curated sampling).\n- Alternative arm (not default): PPO with frozen-snapshot opponent and learned value head.\n- Reward signals (assumed defaults): Taboo +1 win / 0 otherwise (taboo violations auxiliary diagnostic, not in reward); Q20 +1 asker-correct / 0 otherwise; City +1 correct / 0 otherwise.\n- Episode cap T = 20 turns (sweep over {10, 20, 40}).\n- Episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes on the rolling winner pool.\n- Output: one post-self-play checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, seed, model_id).\n\n### 4.3 Evaluation phase\nEvery checkpoint evaluated on (a) the locked downstream reasoning suite and (b) a held-out game split (5,000 episodes per game, content disjoint from training/trajectory content).\n\n---\n\n## 5. 7-Cell Ablation Grid\n\n| # | Cell | Imitation | Self-play | Game | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Taboo-impl | yes | — | Taboo | isolates imitation |\n| 2 | Taboo-full (SPAG) | yes | yes | Taboo | primary condition |\n| 3 | Q20-impl | yes | — | 20-Question | |\n| 4 | Q20-full | yes | yes | 20-Question | primary non-adversarial #1 |\n| 5 | City-impl | yes | — | Guess-My-City | |\n| 6 | City-full | yes | yes | Guess-My-City | primary non-adversarial #2 |\n| 7 | Baseline | — | — | — | generic instruct-only FT, same FLOPs |\n\nCells matched on FLOPs, prompts, optimiser (RSFT default), base model, seeds.\n\n**Reduced (bare-minimum) mode:** cells {2, 4, 6} only — three full-game cells.\n\n**Stretch:** add 8th/9th cells using the N=2,500 imitation arm at post-self-play step (Taboo-full-2.5K, Q20-full-2.5K, City-full-2.5K) for data-efficiency probe, plus a 10th cell using the League opponent arm at Taboo-full.\n\n**Pre-registered primary contrasts (3):**\n1. Taboo-full vs. Q20-full (macro-Δ).\n2. Taboo-full vs. City-full (macro-Δ).\n3. Q20-full vs. City-full (macro-Δ).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Controls and Matching\n\n- Base model: family-agnostic; default Llama-3-8B-Instruct (fallback Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct). Scaling probe: 13B-class (Llama-3-13B-Instruct or Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct), treated as model_id random effect.\n- Compute: FLOP-matched to ±2% across cells. If Taboo needs more tokens per episode due to taboo-list conditioning, equalize per-episode token budgets (default) or cap Taboo self-play episodes (sensitivity arm).\n- Imitation data: N_imitation matched across game cells (10,000 default).\n- Self-play episodes: matched (20,000 default).\n- Optimizer hyperparameters: AdamW, cosine LR, identical peak LR (joint sweep), warm-up, batch size, gradient clipping, precision (bf16/fp32 mixed).\n- Prompts: identical scaffolding; game-specific content swapped. System-token budget matched to ±5%.\n- Decoding during evaluation: temperature 0; max-new-tokens cap; identical across cells.\n- Few-shot: zero-shot on all four downstream suites; locked.\n- Seeds: ≥3, target 5, identically seeded across cells (paired seed design).\n- Game-content pools: Taboo word pool ≥5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs; Q20 object pool ≥5,000 distinct common-noun targets; City pool ≥5,000 cities. All disjoint from reasoning-benchmark text (audit gates training; §11).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evaluation Suite\n\nLocked prompts and decoding settings, versioned alongside the design.\n\n- BBH: all 23 subtasks; canonical prompt per subtask; macro-average.\n- GSM8K: zero-shot CoT with locked prompt; optional 8-shot sensitivity.\n- ARC-Challenge: test split; zero-shot; canonical prompt.\n- MMLU-STEM: zero-shot; subject-level macro-average.\n\nHeld-out game split: 5,000 episodes per game, content disjoint from training/trajectory content; reports success rate, mean turns-to-success, (Taboo) taboo-violation rate.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Statistical Analysis Plan\n\n### 8.1 Pre-registered model\n\nMixed-effects model, fit per benchmark and pooled:\n\nΔ_acc ~ condition + (1 | seed) + (1 | model_id)\n\nWhere condition is the 7-cell grid (or 3-cell subset for primary inference).\n\n### 8.2 Primary contrasts and α\n- 3 primary contrasts at α = 0.05 family-wise; Holm–Bonferroni correction across the three.\n- Reported with adjusted p-values; raw p-values in appendix.\n- Effect size: paired Cohen's d (cell-by-seed within model_id); 95% CI by 10,000-resample bootstrap at the seed level, stratified by model_id.\n\n### 8.3 Power and sample size\n- Target: ≥3 seeds, ideally 5.\n- With 5 seeds at one model size and within-cell σ_Δ ~5 pp, 80% power to detect d ≈ 0.55 (~2.75 pp). With 3 seeds, detectable d ≈ 0.85.\n- Failure-to-reject does not equal evidence of equality; report as such.\n- If only 3 seeds feasible, skip the scaling probe.\n\n### 8.4 Supporting contrasts (secondary, exploratory)\n- Within each game: Full vs. Impl-only delta.\n- Game-impl vs. Baseline.\n- League-opponent Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (sensitivity).\n- PPO Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (optimizer arm).\n- 7B vs. 13B interaction on Taboo-full vs. Q20-full (scaling probe).\n- Held-out game performance (overfitting check).\n- Mechanistic diagnostic (§9).\n\nAll supporting contrasts reported with unadjusted p-values and labelled exploratory.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Mechanistic Diagnostics\n\nRequired to distinguish \"adversarial pressure drives transfer\" from \"self-play drives transfer\":\n\n- Self-play reward → downstream Δ correlation: per cell, plot rolling success rate (last 500 episodes) against rolling checkpoint downstream Δ; Spearman ρ between reward slope and downstream Δ slope. **Locked expectation if H1a is true:** ρ positive for Taboo, small or null for Q20 and City.\n- Taboo-violation rate trajectory: the canonical mechanistic trace. Sustained non-trivial rate is consistent with sustained adversarial pressure; collapse to zero early undermines H1a.\n- Turns-to-success trajectory.\n- Discriminative probe (optional): zero-shot transfer to a held-out *adversarial* reasoning task (adversarial Cloze, give-away puzzles). Reported only as exploratory.\n\nThese diagnostics are pre-registered as mandatory regardless of headline outcome.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Assumptions Block (every inferred SPAG detail enumerated)\n\nNo item below is a citation of SPAG paper text. Each is a parameter fixed by this design, replaceable by a researcher-supplied value.\n\nSPAG operational specifics (inferred defaults):\n- A1. Base student model: Llama-3-8B-Instruct (fallback Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct); scaling probe at 13B-class.\n- A2. Self-play optimiser: RSFT (default); alternative arm PPO; optional arm league+replay-buffer.\n- A3. Opponent-sampling: frozen self-snapshot every K=500 episodes (default); K swept over {100, 500, 2000}.\n- A4. Episode cap T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}.\n- A5. Self-play reward: sparse +1 win / 0 otherwise; taboo-violation rate tracked but not reward-scored.\n- A6. Imitation corpus size N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 ablation arm.\n- A7. GPT-4 prompting template: CoT-style system prompt with identical scaffolding across games, only game-specific content varied.\n- A8. Phase-A optimizer: AdamW with cosine LR; peak LR swept jointly across cells over {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}.\n- A9. Self-play episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes.\n- A10. Taboo word pool size ≥5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs.\n- A11. Number of seeds ≥3, target 5.\n- A12. Identical prompt scaffolding across games within ±5% system-token budget.\n\nFraming assumptions (locked by the design):\n- A13. 20-Question is non-adversarial / cooperative — single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target.\n- A14. Guess-My-City is non-adversarial — single agent, no opponent utility.\n- A15. Taboo is adversarial — asymmetric role pair with opposing incentives.\n- A16. \"Reasoning improvement\" = macro-aggregated zero-shot Δ across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM.\n\nReasoning-benchmark assumptions:\n- A17. BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style evaluations are commensurate with it.\n- A18. ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination with game content pools (verified by §11 audit).\n\nCompute assumptions:\n- A19. Compute expressible in 8 × A100-80G node GPU-hours.\n- A20. Sustained training throughput ≈150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32 mixed) for 7–13B transformer training.\n\nInferential assumptions:\n- A21. Per-cell Δ-noise σ_Δ ≤ 5 pp across seeds for primary benchmarks (from LLM-evaluation literature).\n- A22. Sensitivity: detectable d ≥ 0.55 (5 seeds) / d ≥ 0.85 (3 seeds) at 80% power, α = 0.05.\n- A23. Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is a random-effects meta-analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Threats to Validity and Prompt-Leakage Audit\n\nInternal:\n- T1. Spurious adversarial signal: taboo-list terms overlapping BBH; mitigated by A10/A18 and the audit.\n- T2. In-game overfitting under RSFT: mitigated by held-out game split and reward-vs-Δ plot.\n- T3. Prompt-template confound: mitigated by A12.\n- T4. Default-taboo overlap with benchmarks: tested by the audit.\n\nConstruct:\n- T5. Operationalisation of \"adversarial\" — secondary arm (Q20-as-zero-sum) added at researcher discretion if SPAG source treats it adversarially.\n- T6. Reward-shaping — taboo-violation term excluded from default reward; sensitivity arm includes -0.1 per violation.\n- T7. Optimiser confound — RSFT default, PPO and league arms available.\n\nExternal:\n- T8. Model-family generalisation — scaling probe at 13B; family effect in mixed model.\n\nPrompt-leakage audit (mandatory, gates training):\n1. Token overlap check: 1,000 trajectories + 1,000 GPT-4 imitation completions per cell; (a) exact substring overlap of taboo words / Q20 targets / cities with BBH, GSM8K, ARC, MMLU items; (b) 8-gram exact match. Any hit triggers content-pool revision.\n2. Standardised contamination screen: post-imitation checkpoint run zero-shot on benchmark subsets and reverse-engineered dummy items; non-trivial accuracy differences trigger revision.\n3. Train/test split discipline: content split, not surface split; overlapping candidates move to discard set.\n4. Persona check: GPT-4 system prompt forbids terms that would solve benchmark items verbatim; system prompt locked and audited on 200 sampled generations.\n\nFailure of any audit step blocks launch and requires content-pool revision.\n\nRubin's rules (for pooling model sizes): mixed-effects model includes model_id as random effect (§8.1); pooled point estimate Σ_k w_k θ̂_k with w_k = 1/σ̂_k²; pooled variance Σ_k w_k² σ̂_k² with cluster-robust bootstrap over seeds. Pooled estimates are secondary to model-family-specific estimates.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Pre-registration Checklist\n\n- [ ] Hypothesis registration (H0/H1a/H1b/H0-imitation/H0-selfplay/H0-game), omnibus model, contrasts, α, power.\n- [ ] 7-cell grid with cell-defining axes (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, T, optimiser).\n- [ ] 3 primary contrasts (Taboo−Q20, Taboo−City, Q20−City) with Holm–Bonferroni.\n- [ ] Base-model default and scaling probe fixed (with re-parameterisation note).\n- [ ] Self-play optimiser default (RSFT) and frozen-snapshot opponent policy fixed; alternative arms documented.\n- [ ] Imitation corpus default (10,000) and 2,500 arm fixed.\n- [ ] Prompt-scaffolding match-check across games (A12).\n- [ ] Compute envelope 8 × A100-80G node, FLOP-matched (A19/A20); 7-cell vs reduced-3-cell budget table.\n- [ ] Seed plan (≥3, target 5).\n- [ ] Downstream suite: BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM; zero-shot, locked prompts.\n- [ ] Reporting: Δ from post-imitation; per-benchmark + macro-averaged; absolute-from-base supplementary.\n- [ ] Mechanistic diagnostics: reward-vs-Δ correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory, turns-to-success trajectory.\n- [ ] Prompt-leakage audit (T1–T4 / §11 steps 1–4) with gate-before-train.\n- [ ] Assumptions block (A1–A23) signed off by researcher.\n- [ ] Threats-to-validity (T1–T8) signed off.\n- [ ] Hold-out split of game content and benchmark content, versioned and immutable.\n- [ ] Rubin pooling procedure for multi-model-size secondary estimate.\n- [ ] Decoding settings, prompt templates, few-shot settings locked.\n- [ ] Random seeds enumerated in advance for each cell × model.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Compute Envelope Coarse Budget Table (illustrative; 8 × A100-80G node)\n\nFLOP estimates use 6N per token for forward+backward (training), 2N for forward only (rollouts), at sustained 150 TFLOPs/node.\n\n| Cell | Imitation FLOPs | Self-play FLOPs | Eval FLOPs | Node-hours (8B) | Node-hours (13B) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Taboo-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\n| Taboo-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\n| Q20-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\n| Q20-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\n| City-impl | 5.5e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.0 | ~1.7 |\n| City-full | 5.5e17 | 4.0e18 | 1.6e13 | ~8.5 | ~14.0 |\n| Baseline | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\n| Full factorial, 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~165 node-hours | |\n| Full factorial, 5 seeds, 13B | | | | | ~275 node-hours |\n| Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~145 node-hours | |\n| Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 13B | | | | | ~235 node-hours |\n| Stretch (full factorial + 2.5K + league arms), 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~250 node-hours | |\n\nTrajectory token budgets: Taboo ≈ 1.5K, Q20 ≈ 2K, City ≈ 0.5K; self-play episode ≈ 2× that. Cell-count × seed-count factored into totals; model-id sweeps multiply by 2.\n\nIf compute is tight: reduced 3-cell × 5 seeds × 8B (≈145 node-hours). If even that is infeasible, drop to 3 seeds (≈85 node-hours).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Pipeline Summary (TL;DR for the design file)\n\nA base open LM (default 8B; scaling probe 13B) is SFT'd on 10,000 GPT-4-played trajectories per game (Taboo, 20-Question, Guess-My-City) using a shared prompt scaffold (imitation-only cells); the post-imitation checkpoint then enters RSFT self-play against a frozen self-snapshot refreshed every K=500 over 20,000 episodes (full cells). A 7-cell ablation grid (Taboo/Q20/City × Full/Impl-only plus Baseline) is trained and evaluated; primary contrasts Taboo−Q20, Taboo−City, Q20−City tested at α = 0.05 family-wise with Holm–Bonferroni; effect sizes as paired Cohen's d with 10,000-resample bootstrap CIs; mechanistic diagnostics (reward-vs-Δ correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory) are pre-registered as mandatory. Compute FLOP-matched across cells within ±2%; prompt-leakage audit gates training; reporting per-benchmark and macro-averaged Δ from post-imitation; pooled multi-model-size estimates follow Rubin's rules. /workspace contains only task.json; no SPAG paper text is imported; all SPAG operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters (§10).\n\n---\n\n## 15. What is intentionally not in this design\n- No fabricated experimental outcome. The design predicts expected effect size for power calculation but reports no empirical finding.\n- No claim of execution. Outputs of GPT-4, model checkpoints, or downstream metric values are not asserted.\n- No invocation of network or external APIs. The bubblewrap runtime precludes both.\n- No citation of SPAG paper text not present in the supplied context. Every operational fact is an inferred default in §10.", "evidence": "Repository evidence: (a) /workspace contains exactly one file task.json (verified via ls -la /workspace and find /workspace -maxdepth 3); (b) task.json body, read via inspect_repository, contains the research question, a \"design-only\" context line, and three researcher topic IDs neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2, setup_32, setup_33; the researcher has confirmed the topics list is empty and the bodies cannot be supplied; (c) find / -iname \"*spag*\" and find / -iname \"*taboo*\" returned no relevant hits — no SPAG/Taboo paper or code on disk. Environment evidence (from task contract): bubblewrap runtime; network disabled; gpu_enabled false; cpu_affinity_count 24; project dependencies not preinstalled; datasets empty; note: successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment. Researcher-side evidence (latest message): explicit approval of the approach with five priority resolutions (20-Question fixed as non-adversarial cooperative; model-family-agnostic with 8B default and 13B scaling probe; BBH/GSM8K/ARC/MMLU-STEM as primary suite; RSFT default with PPO and league as arms; 10K imitation default with 2.5K ablation; compute in 8xA100-80G node-hours) and seven guardrails (cooperative-framing explicitness, prompt-leakage audit, mechanistic diagnostics, pre-registered mixed-effects with Holm-Bonferroni over three primary contrasts and Cohen's d with bootstrap CIs, feasibility and threat-to-validity with Rubin pooling, fifteen-section structure, stop-without-execution). The design reflects every guardrail in a corresponding numbered section. No SPAG paper text is asserted; every value labelled \"default\" or \"sweep\" is a design-fixed parameter.", "assumptions": ["A1. Base student model is Llama-3-8B-Instruct (fallback Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct); scaling probe at 13B-class. — Design parameter, not source-anchored.</item>\n<item>A2. Self-play optimizer default is Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT); PPO and league+replay-buffer are arms. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A3. Opponent-sampling default is a frozen self-snapshot refreshed every K=500 episodes; K swept over {100, 500, 2000}. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A4. Episode cap T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A5. Self-play reward is sparse +1 win / 0 otherwise; taboo-violation rate is a diagnostic, not a reward term. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A6. Imitation corpus size N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 ablation arm. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A7. GPT-4 system prompt is CoT-style and uses identical scaffolding across games, varying only game-specific content. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A8. Phase-A optimizer is AdamW with cosine LR; peak LR swept jointly across cells over {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A9. Self-play episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A10. Taboo word pool size >= 5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs, disjoint from reasoning benchmarks. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A11. Number of seeds >= 3, target 5. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A12. Identical prompt scaffolding across games within +/- 5% system-token budget. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A13. 20-Question is fixed as non-adversarial / cooperative (single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target). — Locked framing choice per researcher.</item>\n<item>A14. Guess-My-City is non-adversarial (single agent, no opponent utility). — Locked framing choice.</item>\n<item>A15. Taboo is adversarial (asymmetric role pair, opposing incentives, taboo-avoidance constraint). — Locked framing choice.</item>\n<item>A16. \"Reasoning improvement\" is measured by macro-aggregated zero-shot Delta across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM. — Locked by primary-suite selection.</item>\n<item>A17. BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style scores are commensurate with it. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A18. ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination with game content pools; verified by the Section 11 audit which gates training. — Verified-not-asserted.</item>\n<item>A19. Compute is expressible in 8 x A100-80G node GPU-hours. — Design parameter.</item>\n<item>A20. Sustained training throughput approximately 150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32 mixed) for 7-13B transformer training including backward. — Design parameter for the budget table.</item>\n<item>A21. Per-cell Delta-noise sigma_Delta <= 5 percentage points across seeds for primary benchmarks. — Inference assumption from LLM-evaluation literature.</item>\n<item>A22. Sensitivity: detectable d >= 0.55 (5 seeds) / d >= 0.85 (3 seeds) at 80% power, alpha = 0.05. — Power-derivation assumption.</item>\n<item>A23. Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is a random-effects meta-analysis. — Statistical-modeling assumption.</item>"], "uncertainties": ["Whether ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have any n-gram overlap with Taboo / Q20 / City content pools after the prompt-leakage audit — to be verified before launch; the audit gates training.</item>\n<item>Whether a 13B-class checkpoint fits in the eventual compute envelope alongside the 7-8B default — unknown; the scaling probe is optional and skipped if only 3 seeds are feasible.</item>\n<item>Exact compute envelope available to the experimenter — unknown; the design expresses all sizing in FLOPs and 8xA100-80G node-hours and gives bare-minimum (~85 node-hours, 3 seeds, reduced) and stretch (~250 node-hours with 2.5K and league arms) budgets.</item>\n<item>Whether SPAG reports seed-paired statistics (needed for paired Cohen's d) or only point estimates — supply-bound; the design assumes seed-level variance is recoverable; if not, the analysis degrades to unpaired Welch's t with adjusted CIs.</item>\n<item>SPAG's reported per-benchmark numbers — supply-bound; the design reports both per-benchmark and macro-averaged deltas to align with whatever the original paper reports.</item>\n<item>SPAG's exact GPT-4 prompting template (system prompt wording, CoT policy, role formatting) — supply-bound; design uses identical scaffolding with game-specific content swaps to neutralise template confounds.</item>\n<item>SPAG's exact episode length / turn cap distribution — supply-bound; default T=20 with a sweep.</item>\n<item>SPAG's exact opponent-sampling scheme (PSRO league, replay buffer, ELO-curated, frozen-snapshot) — supply-bound; the default frozen-snapshot is mirrored and league added as an optional arm.</item>\n<item>SPAG's exact reward structure (sparse vs dense, partial credit for taboo violations, dense vs sparse in Q20) — supply-bound; the default sparse +1/0 is replaced by a one-parameter edit if the researcher supplies a value.</item>\n<item>Whether SPAG's own published framing of 20-Question is adversarial or cooperative — supply-bound; if adversarial, an additional sensitivity arm (Q20-as-zero-sum) is added at the researcher's discretion and the published contrast is reported with a framing discrepancy note in Section 11.</item>"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-10b9e02b-8f5f-405b-b25f-4331891dfe6d", "tool_call": {"id": "call-10b9e02b-8f5f-405b-b25f-4331891dfe6d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Deliver full experiment design (clean)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Final Experiment Design \\u2014 SPAG Adversarial vs Non-Adversarial Self-Play for Reasoning\\n\\n**Status:** Design-only. No execution is claimed, no model weights are produced, no network or model APIs are invoked. All operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters and labelled as assumptions; the design is robust to reasonable variations in those parameters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Operating frame: what \\\"non-adversarial\\\" means in this design\\n\\nPer locked design choice, **20-Question and Guess-My-City have no adversary and no zero-sum incentive**. Specifically:\\n\\n- 20-Question: a single asymmetric role pair \\u2014 the asker elicits and the answerer answers truthfully toward a common hidden target. The answerer is rewarded for helping the asker succeed; there is no opposing side penalising information disclosure. Success = asker identifies the target within the turn budget.\\n- Guess-My-City: a single agent that narrows a hidden city through its own questions/guesses. No counter-player, no opponent utility.\\n- Taboo (SPAG): an asymmetric role pair with opposing incentives \\u2014 the describer must convey a target word without uttering any item from a taboo list, while a guesser attempts to name the target.\\n\\n**Assumption (locked):** \\\"Adversarial\\\" = Taboo alone; the contrast is Taboo vs. {Q20, City}. If SPAG's source treats 20-Question as zero-sum, that fact is not in the supplied context; this would be reported as a framing discrepancy in \\u00a711.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Background and Question\\n\\n**Background.** SPAG's pipeline, as described in the supplied task context, has two stages: (i) imitation learning of GPT-4 on game trajectories, and (ii) self-play on game episodes, with the Adversarial Taboo game as the source of \\\"adversarial pressure\\\".\\n\\n**Question.** Does SPAG (imitation + adversarial Taboo self-play) yield larger downstream reasoning gains than imitation + non-adversarial self-play on 20-Question or Guess-My-City, holding all other variables fixed?\\n\\n**Why this matters.** A positive answer implicates adversarial pressure as the active ingredient rather than self-play generally. A null answer implicates self-play *per se* (or imitation alone) as the active ingredient.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Hypotheses\\n\\n- H0 (omnibus null): Under FLOP-matched training, downstream reasoning \\u0394-accuracy is the same across the three self-play regimes (Taboo-full, Q20-full, City-full).\\n- H1a (primary, directional): Taboo-full > Q20-full and Taboo-full > City-full on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\\n- H1b (secondary, directional): Q20-full > City-full.\\n- H0-imitation: Imitation-only checkpoints (Taboo-impl, Q20-impl, City-impl) do not differ across games on macro-averaged reasoning deltas.\\n- H0-selfplay: Within each game, the Full vs. Impl-only delta is zero.\\n- H0-game: The no-game baseline is indistinguishable from any Impl-only condition.\\n\\nPrimary inference targets H1a; everything else is supporting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Independent and Dependent Variables\\n\\n**Independent variable.** Self-play environment, 7-cell ablation grid; primary subset is the 3-cell full-only contrast.\\n\\n**Downstream reasoning (primary, locked-prompt, zero-shot):**\\n- BBH (BIG-Bench Hard) \\u2014 headline; macro-averaged across 23 subtasks.\\n- GSM8K \\u2014 multi-step arithmetic; strict-match accuracy.\\n- ARC-Challenge \\u2014 scientific reasoning; accuracy.\\n- MMLU-STEM \\u2014 secondary general check; accuracy.\\n\\nReporting: per-benchmark and macro-averaged \\u0394 (mean of four normalised per-benchmark \\u0394s).\\n\\n**In-game diagnostics (mechanistic, secondary):**\\n- Episode success rate (per game); mean turns-to-success; (Taboo) taboo-violation rate; self-play reward trajectory.\\n\\nReporting convention: downstream reported as \\u0394 from the **post-imitation** checkpoint of the corresponding game. Absolute scores (vs. base model) reported alongside.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Pipeline (Imitation + Self-Play)\\n\\n### 4.1 Phase A \\u2014 Imitation\\n- Teacher: GPT-4.\\n- Default N_imitation = 10,000 trajectories per game, with a 2,500-trajectory ablation arm (\\u00a75).\\n- GPT-4 plays both sides under a CoT-style system prompt; the student's role transcript is the training target. Cross-game prompt template reuse \\u2014 same scaffolding (system instructions, turn markers), game-specific content swapped. This is the principal control for prompt-format confounds.\\n- Student optimisation: SFT / next-token cross-entropy; 2\\u20133 epochs; AdamW; cosine LR schedule; peak LR \\u2208 {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5} swept jointly across cells.\\n- Output: one post-imitation checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, model_id, seed).\\n\\n### 4.2 Phase B \\u2014 Self-Play\\n- Default optimiser: Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT) \\u2014 keep winning trajectories, fine-tune on them; frozen-snapshot opponent.\\n- Opponent-sampling default: frozen copy of the student, snapshotted every K episodes (default K=500; sweep over {100, 500, 2000}).\\n- Optional arm: League / replay-buffer opponent pool (ensemble of past snapshots with ELO-curated sampling).\\n- Alternative arm (not default): PPO with frozen-snapshot opponent and learned value head.\\n- Reward signals (assumed defaults): Taboo +1 win / 0 otherwise (taboo violations auxiliary diagnostic, not in reward); Q20 +1 asker-correct / 0 otherwise; City +1 correct / 0 otherwise.\\n- Episode cap T = 20 turns (sweep over {10, 20, 40}).\\n- Episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes on the rolling winner pool.\\n- Output: one post-self-play checkpoint per (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, seed, model_id).\\n\\n### 4.3 Evaluation phase\\nEvery checkpoint evaluated on (a) the locked downstream reasoning suite and (b) a held-out game split (5,000 episodes per game, content disjoint from training/trajectory content).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. 7-Cell Ablation Grid\\n\\n| # | Cell | Imitation | Self-play | Game | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Taboo-impl | yes | \\u2014 | Taboo | isolates imitation |\\n| 2 | Taboo-full (SPAG) | yes | yes | Taboo | primary condition |\\n| 3 | Q20-impl | yes | \\u2014 | 20-Question | |\\n| 4 | Q20-full | yes | yes | 20-Question | primary non-adversarial #1 |\\n| 5 | City-impl | yes | \\u2014 | Guess-My-City | |\\n| 6 | City-full | yes | yes | Guess-My-City | primary non-adversarial #2 |\\n| 7 | Baseline | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | generic instruct-only FT, same FLOPs |\\n\\nCells matched on FLOPs, prompts, optimiser (RSFT default), base model, seeds.\\n\\n**Reduced (bare-minimum) mode:** cells {2, 4, 6} only \\u2014 three full-game cells.\\n\\n**Stretch:** add 8th/9th cells using the N=2,500 imitation arm at post-self-play step (Taboo-full-2.5K, Q20-full-2.5K, City-full-2.5K) for data-efficiency probe, plus a 10th cell using the League opponent arm at Taboo-full.\\n\\n**Pre-registered primary contrasts (3):**\\n1. Taboo-full vs. Q20-full (macro-\\u0394).\\n2. Taboo-full vs. City-full (macro-\\u0394).\\n3. Q20-full vs. City-full (macro-\\u0394).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Controls and Matching\\n\\n- Base model: family-agnostic; default Llama-3-8B-Instruct (fallback Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct). Scaling probe: 13B-class (Llama-3-13B-Instruct or Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct), treated as model_id random effect.\\n- Compute: FLOP-matched to \\u00b12% across cells. If Taboo needs more tokens per episode due to taboo-list conditioning, equalize per-episode token budgets (default) or cap Taboo self-play episodes (sensitivity arm).\\n- Imitation data: N_imitation matched across game cells (10,000 default).\\n- Self-play episodes: matched (20,000 default).\\n- Optimizer hyperparameters: AdamW, cosine LR, identical peak LR (joint sweep), warm-up, batch size, gradient clipping, precision (bf16/fp32 mixed).\\n- Prompts: identical scaffolding; game-specific content swapped. System-token budget matched to \\u00b15%.\\n- Decoding during evaluation: temperature 0; max-new-tokens cap; identical across cells.\\n- Few-shot: zero-shot on all four downstream suites; locked.\\n- Seeds: \\u22653, target 5, identically seeded across cells (paired seed design).\\n- Game-content pools: Taboo word pool \\u22655,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs; Q20 object pool \\u22655,000 distinct common-noun targets; City pool \\u22655,000 cities. All disjoint from reasoning-benchmark text (audit gates training; \\u00a711).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Evaluation Suite\\n\\nLocked prompts and decoding settings, versioned alongside the design.\\n\\n- BBH: all 23 subtasks; canonical prompt per subtask; macro-average.\\n- GSM8K: zero-shot CoT with locked prompt; optional 8-shot sensitivity.\\n- ARC-Challenge: test split; zero-shot; canonical prompt.\\n- MMLU-STEM: zero-shot; subject-level macro-average.\\n\\nHeld-out game split: 5,000 episodes per game, content disjoint from training/trajectory content; reports success rate, mean turns-to-success, (Taboo) taboo-violation rate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Statistical Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 8.1 Pre-registered model\\n\\nMixed-effects model, fit per benchmark and pooled:\\n\\n\\u0394_acc ~ condition + (1 | seed) + (1 | model_id)\\n\\nWhere condition is the 7-cell grid (or 3-cell subset for primary inference).\\n\\n### 8.2 Primary contrasts and \\u03b1\\n- 3 primary contrasts at \\u03b1 = 0.05 family-wise; Holm\\u2013Bonferroni correction across the three.\\n- Reported with adjusted p-values; raw p-values in appendix.\\n- Effect size: paired Cohen's d (cell-by-seed within model_id); 95% CI by 10,000-resample bootstrap at the seed level, stratified by model_id.\\n\\n### 8.3 Power and sample size\\n- Target: \\u22653 seeds, ideally 5.\\n- With 5 seeds at one model size and within-cell \\u03c3_\\u0394 ~5 pp, 80% power to detect d \\u2248 0.55 (~2.75 pp). With 3 seeds, detectable d \\u2248 0.85.\\n- Failure-to-reject does not equal evidence of equality; report as such.\\n- If only 3 seeds feasible, skip the scaling probe.\\n\\n### 8.4 Supporting contrasts (secondary, exploratory)\\n- Within each game: Full vs. Impl-only delta.\\n- Game-impl vs. Baseline.\\n- League-opponent Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (sensitivity).\\n- PPO Taboo-full vs. RSFT Taboo-full (optimizer arm).\\n- 7B vs. 13B interaction on Taboo-full vs. Q20-full (scaling probe).\\n- Held-out game performance (overfitting check).\\n- Mechanistic diagnostic (\\u00a79).\\n\\nAll supporting contrasts reported with unadjusted p-values and labelled exploratory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Mechanistic Diagnostics\\n\\nRequired to distinguish \\\"adversarial pressure drives transfer\\\" from \\\"self-play drives transfer\\\":\\n\\n- Self-play reward \\u2192 downstream \\u0394 correlation: per cell, plot rolling success rate (last 500 episodes) against rolling checkpoint downstream \\u0394; Spearman \\u03c1 between reward slope and downstream \\u0394 slope. **Locked expectation if H1a is true:** \\u03c1 positive for Taboo, small or null for Q20 and City.\\n- Taboo-violation rate trajectory: the canonical mechanistic trace. Sustained non-trivial rate is consistent with sustained adversarial pressure; collapse to zero early undermines H1a.\\n- Turns-to-success trajectory.\\n- Discriminative probe (optional): zero-shot transfer to a held-out *adversarial* reasoning task (adversarial Cloze, give-away puzzles). Reported only as exploratory.\\n\\nThese diagnostics are pre-registered as mandatory regardless of headline outcome.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions Block (every inferred SPAG detail enumerated)\\n\\nNo item below is a citation of SPAG paper text. Each is a parameter fixed by this design, replaceable by a researcher-supplied value.\\n\\nSPAG operational specifics (inferred defaults):\\n- A1. Base student model: Llama-3-8B-Instruct (fallback Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct); scaling probe at 13B-class.\\n- A2. Self-play optimiser: RSFT (default); alternative arm PPO; optional arm league+replay-buffer.\\n- A3. Opponent-sampling: frozen self-snapshot every K=500 episodes (default); K swept over {100, 500, 2000}.\\n- A4. Episode cap T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}.\\n- A5. Self-play reward: sparse +1 win / 0 otherwise; taboo-violation rate tracked but not reward-scored.\\n- A6. Imitation corpus size N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 ablation arm.\\n- A7. GPT-4 prompting template: CoT-style system prompt with identical scaffolding across games, only game-specific content varied.\\n- A8. Phase-A optimizer: AdamW with cosine LR; peak LR swept jointly across cells over {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}.\\n- A9. Self-play episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes.\\n- A10. Taboo word pool size \\u22655,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs.\\n- A11. Number of seeds \\u22653, target 5.\\n- A12. Identical prompt scaffolding across games within \\u00b15% system-token budget.\\n\\nFraming assumptions (locked by the design):\\n- A13. 20-Question is non-adversarial / cooperative \\u2014 single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target.\\n- A14. Guess-My-City is non-adversarial \\u2014 single agent, no opponent utility.\\n- A15. Taboo is adversarial \\u2014 asymmetric role pair with opposing incentives.\\n- A16. \\\"Reasoning improvement\\\" = macro-aggregated zero-shot \\u0394 across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM.\\n\\nReasoning-benchmark assumptions:\\n- A17. BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style evaluations are commensurate with it.\\n- A18. ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination with game content pools (verified by \\u00a711 audit).\\n\\nCompute assumptions:\\n- A19. Compute expressible in 8 \\u00d7 A100-80G node GPU-hours.\\n- A20. Sustained training throughput \\u2248150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32 mixed) for 7\\u201313B transformer training.\\n\\nInferential assumptions:\\n- A21. Per-cell \\u0394-noise \\u03c3_\\u0394 \\u2264 5 pp across seeds for primary benchmarks (from LLM-evaluation literature).\\n- A22. Sensitivity: detectable d \\u2265 0.55 (5 seeds) / d \\u2265 0.85 (3 seeds) at 80% power, \\u03b1 = 0.05.\\n- A23. Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is a random-effects meta-analysis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Threats to Validity and Prompt-Leakage Audit\\n\\nInternal:\\n- T1. Spurious adversarial signal: taboo-list terms overlapping BBH; mitigated by A10/A18 and the audit.\\n- T2. In-game overfitting under RSFT: mitigated by held-out game split and reward-vs-\\u0394 plot.\\n- T3. Prompt-template confound: mitigated by A12.\\n- T4. Default-taboo overlap with benchmarks: tested by the audit.\\n\\nConstruct:\\n- T5. Operationalisation of \\\"adversarial\\\" \\u2014 secondary arm (Q20-as-zero-sum) added at researcher discretion if SPAG source treats it adversarially.\\n- T6. Reward-shaping \\u2014 taboo-violation term excluded from default reward; sensitivity arm includes -0.1 per violation.\\n- T7. Optimiser confound \\u2014 RSFT default, PPO and league arms available.\\n\\nExternal:\\n- T8. Model-family generalisation \\u2014 scaling probe at 13B; family effect in mixed model.\\n\\nPrompt-leakage audit (mandatory, gates training):\\n1. Token overlap check: 1,000 trajectories + 1,000 GPT-4 imitation completions per cell; (a) exact substring overlap of taboo words / Q20 targets / cities with BBH, GSM8K, ARC, MMLU items; (b) 8-gram exact match. Any hit triggers content-pool revision.\\n2. Standardised contamination screen: post-imitation checkpoint run zero-shot on benchmark subsets and reverse-engineered dummy items; non-trivial accuracy differences trigger revision.\\n3. Train/test split discipline: content split, not surface split; overlapping candidates move to discard set.\\n4. Persona check: GPT-4 system prompt forbids terms that would solve benchmark items verbatim; system prompt locked and audited on 200 sampled generations.\\n\\nFailure of any audit step blocks launch and requires content-pool revision.\\n\\nRubin's rules (for pooling model sizes): mixed-effects model includes model_id as random effect (\\u00a78.1); pooled point estimate \\u03a3_k w_k \\u03b8\\u0302_k with w_k = 1/\\u03c3\\u0302_k\\u00b2; pooled variance \\u03a3_k w_k\\u00b2 \\u03c3\\u0302_k\\u00b2 with cluster-robust bootstrap over seeds. Pooled estimates are secondary to model-family-specific estimates.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Pre-registration Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Hypothesis registration (H0/H1a/H1b/H0-imitation/H0-selfplay/H0-game), omnibus model, contrasts, \\u03b1, power.\\n- [ ] 7-cell grid with cell-defining axes (game, N_imitation, opponent scheme, K, T, optimiser).\\n- [ ] 3 primary contrasts (Taboo\\u2212Q20, Taboo\\u2212City, Q20\\u2212City) with Holm\\u2013Bonferroni.\\n- [ ] Base-model default and scaling probe fixed (with re-parameterisation note).\\n- [ ] Self-play optimiser default (RSFT) and frozen-snapshot opponent policy fixed; alternative arms documented.\\n- [ ] Imitation corpus default (10,000) and 2,500 arm fixed.\\n- [ ] Prompt-scaffolding match-check across games (A12).\\n- [ ] Compute envelope 8 \\u00d7 A100-80G node, FLOP-matched (A19/A20); 7-cell vs reduced-3-cell budget table.\\n- [ ] Seed plan (\\u22653, target 5).\\n- [ ] Downstream suite: BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM; zero-shot, locked prompts.\\n- [ ] Reporting: \\u0394 from post-imitation; per-benchmark + macro-averaged; absolute-from-base supplementary.\\n- [ ] Mechanistic diagnostics: reward-vs-\\u0394 correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory, turns-to-success trajectory.\\n- [ ] Prompt-leakage audit (T1\\u2013T4 / \\u00a711 steps 1\\u20134) with gate-before-train.\\n- [ ] Assumptions block (A1\\u2013A23) signed off by researcher.\\n- [ ] Threats-to-validity (T1\\u2013T8) signed off.\\n- [ ] Hold-out split of game content and benchmark content, versioned and immutable.\\n- [ ] Rubin pooling procedure for multi-model-size secondary estimate.\\n- [ ] Decoding settings, prompt templates, few-shot settings locked.\\n- [ ] Random seeds enumerated in advance for each cell \\u00d7 model.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Compute Envelope Coarse Budget Table (illustrative; 8 \\u00d7 A100-80G node)\\n\\nFLOP estimates use 6N per token for forward+backward (training), 2N for forward only (rollouts), at sustained 150 TFLOPs/node.\\n\\n| Cell | Imitation FLOPs | Self-play FLOPs | Eval FLOPs | Node-hours (8B) | Node-hours (13B) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Taboo-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Taboo-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\\n| Q20-impl | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Q20-full | 7.2e17 | 4.8e18 | 1.6e13 | ~10.0 | ~16.5 |\\n| City-impl | 5.5e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.0 | ~1.7 |\\n| City-full | 5.5e17 | 4.0e18 | 1.6e13 | ~8.5 | ~14.0 |\\n| Baseline | 7.2e17 | 0 | 1.6e13 | ~1.3 | ~2.2 |\\n| Full factorial, 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~165 node-hours | |\\n| Full factorial, 5 seeds, 13B | | | | | ~275 node-hours |\\n| Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~145 node-hours | |\\n| Reduced (3 full-game cells), 5 seeds, 13B | | | | | ~235 node-hours |\\n| Stretch (full factorial + 2.5K + league arms), 5 seeds, 8B | | | | ~250 node-hours | |\\n\\nTrajectory token budgets: Taboo \\u2248 1.5K, Q20 \\u2248 2K, City \\u2248 0.5K; self-play episode \\u2248 2\\u00d7 that. Cell-count \\u00d7 seed-count factored into totals; model-id sweeps multiply by 2.\\n\\nIf compute is tight: reduced 3-cell \\u00d7 5 seeds \\u00d7 8B (\\u2248145 node-hours). If even that is infeasible, drop to 3 seeds (\\u224885 node-hours).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. Pipeline Summary (TL;DR for the design file)\\n\\nA base open LM (default 8B; scaling probe 13B) is SFT'd on 10,000 GPT-4-played trajectories per game (Taboo, 20-Question, Guess-My-City) using a shared prompt scaffold (imitation-only cells); the post-imitation checkpoint then enters RSFT self-play against a frozen self-snapshot refreshed every K=500 over 20,000 episodes (full cells). A 7-cell ablation grid (Taboo/Q20/City \\u00d7 Full/Impl-only plus Baseline) is trained and evaluated; primary contrasts Taboo\\u2212Q20, Taboo\\u2212City, Q20\\u2212City tested at \\u03b1 = 0.05 family-wise with Holm\\u2013Bonferroni; effect sizes as paired Cohen's d with 10,000-resample bootstrap CIs; mechanistic diagnostics (reward-vs-\\u0394 correlation, taboo-violation-rate trajectory) are pre-registered as mandatory. Compute FLOP-matched across cells within \\u00b12%; prompt-leakage audit gates training; reporting per-benchmark and macro-averaged \\u0394 from post-imitation; pooled multi-model-size estimates follow Rubin's rules. /workspace contains only task.json; no SPAG paper text is imported; all SPAG operational specifics are fixed by this design as parameters (\\u00a710).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. What is intentionally not in this design\\n- No fabricated experimental outcome. The design predicts expected effect size for power calculation but reports no empirical finding.\\n- No claim of execution. Outputs of GPT-4, model checkpoints, or downstream metric values are not asserted.\\n- No invocation of network or external APIs. The bubblewrap runtime precludes both.\\n- No citation of SPAG paper text not present in the supplied context. Every operational fact is an inferred default in \\u00a710.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository evidence: (a) /workspace contains exactly one file task.json (verified via ls -la /workspace and find /workspace -maxdepth 3); (b) task.json body, read via inspect_repository, contains the research question, a \\\"design-only\\\" context line, and three researcher topic IDs neurips2024/93638/3:setup_2, setup_32, setup_33; the researcher has confirmed the topics list is empty and the bodies cannot be supplied; (c) find / -iname \\\"*spag*\\\" and find / -iname \\\"*taboo*\\\" returned no relevant hits \\u2014 no SPAG/Taboo paper or code on disk. Environment evidence (from task contract): bubblewrap runtime; network disabled; gpu_enabled false; cpu_affinity_count 24; project dependencies not preinstalled; datasets empty; note: successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment. Researcher-side evidence (latest message): explicit approval of the approach with five priority resolutions (20-Question fixed as non-adversarial cooperative; model-family-agnostic with 8B default and 13B scaling probe; BBH/GSM8K/ARC/MMLU-STEM as primary suite; RSFT default with PPO and league as arms; 10K imitation default with 2.5K ablation; compute in 8xA100-80G node-hours) and seven guardrails (cooperative-framing explicitness, prompt-leakage audit, mechanistic diagnostics, pre-registered mixed-effects with Holm-Bonferroni over three primary contrasts and Cohen's d with bootstrap CIs, feasibility and threat-to-validity with Rubin pooling, fifteen-section structure, stop-without-execution). The design reflects every guardrail in a corresponding numbered section. No SPAG paper text is asserted; every value labelled \\\"default\\\" or \\\"sweep\\\" is a design-fixed parameter.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. Base student model is Llama-3-8B-Instruct (fallback Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct); scaling probe at 13B-class. \\u2014 Design parameter, not source-anchored.</item>\\n<item>A2. Self-play optimizer default is Rejection-Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT); PPO and league+replay-buffer are arms. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A3. Opponent-sampling default is a frozen self-snapshot refreshed every K=500 episodes; K swept over {100, 500, 2000}. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A4. Episode cap T = 20 turns; swept over {10, 20, 40}. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A5. Self-play reward is sparse +1 win / 0 otherwise; taboo-violation rate is a diagnostic, not a reward term. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A6. Imitation corpus size N = 10,000 trajectories per game (default); 2,500 ablation arm. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A7. GPT-4 system prompt is CoT-style and uses identical scaffolding across games, varying only game-specific content. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A8. Phase-A optimizer is AdamW with cosine LR; peak LR swept jointly across cells over {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A9. Self-play episode budget N_selfplay = 20,000 per cell; RSFT refits every R = 2,000 episodes. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A10. Taboo word pool size >= 5,000 (target, taboo-list) pairs, disjoint from reasoning benchmarks. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A11. Number of seeds >= 3, target 5. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A12. Identical prompt scaffolding across games within +/- 5% system-token budget. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A13. 20-Question is fixed as non-adversarial / cooperative (single asymmetric role pair, truthful answerer, shared hidden target). \\u2014 Locked framing choice per researcher.</item>\\n<item>A14. Guess-My-City is non-adversarial (single agent, no opponent utility). \\u2014 Locked framing choice.</item>\\n<item>A15. Taboo is adversarial (asymmetric role pair, opposing incentives, taboo-avoidance constraint). \\u2014 Locked framing choice.</item>\\n<item>A16. \\\"Reasoning improvement\\\" is measured by macro-aggregated zero-shot Delta across BBH, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, MMLU-STEM. \\u2014 Locked by primary-suite selection.</item>\\n<item>A17. BBH is the headline benchmark and SPAG-style scores are commensurate with it. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A18. ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have negligible contamination with game content pools; verified by the Section 11 audit which gates training. \\u2014 Verified-not-asserted.</item>\\n<item>A19. Compute is expressible in 8 x A100-80G node GPU-hours. \\u2014 Design parameter.</item>\\n<item>A20. Sustained training throughput approximately 150 TFLOPs/node (bf16/fp32 mixed) for 7-13B transformer training including backward. \\u2014 Design parameter for the budget table.</item>\\n<item>A21. Per-cell Delta-noise sigma_Delta <= 5 percentage points across seeds for primary benchmarks. \\u2014 Inference assumption from LLM-evaluation literature.</item>\\n<item>A22. Sensitivity: detectable d >= 0.55 (5 seeds) / d >= 0.85 (3 seeds) at 80% power, alpha = 0.05. \\u2014 Power-derivation assumption.</item>\\n<item>A23. Mixed-effects model with seed and model_id as crossed random effects is appropriate; Rubin-style pooling across model sizes is a random-effects meta-analysis. \\u2014 Statistical-modeling assumption.</item>\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether ARC-Challenge and MMLU-STEM have any n-gram overlap with Taboo / Q20 / City content pools after the prompt-leakage audit \\u2014 to be verified before launch; the audit gates training.</item>\\n<item>Whether a 13B-class checkpoint fits in the eventual compute envelope alongside the 7-8B default \\u2014 unknown; the scaling probe is optional and skipped if only 3 seeds are feasible.</item>\\n<item>Exact compute envelope available to the experimenter \\u2014 unknown; the design expresses all sizing in FLOPs and 8xA100-80G node-hours and gives bare-minimum (~85 node-hours, 3 seeds, reduced) and stretch (~250 node-hours with 2.5K and league arms) budgets.</item>\\n<item>Whether SPAG reports seed-paired statistics (needed for paired Cohen's d) or only point estimates \\u2014 supply-bound; the design assumes seed-level variance is recoverable; if not, the analysis degrades to unpaired Welch's t with adjusted CIs.</item>\\n<item>SPAG's reported per-benchmark numbers \\u2014 supply-bound; the design reports both per-benchmark and macro-averaged deltas to align with whatever the original paper reports.</item>\\n<item>SPAG's exact GPT-4 prompting template (system prompt wording, CoT policy, role formatting) \\u2014 supply-bound; design uses identical scaffolding with game-specific content swaps to neutralise template confounds.</item>\\n<item>SPAG's exact episode length / turn cap distribution \\u2014 supply-bound; default T=20 with a sweep.</item>\\n<item>SPAG's exact opponent-sampling scheme (PSRO league, replay buffer, ELO-curated, frozen-snapshot) \\u2014 supply-bound; the default frozen-snapshot is mirrored and league added as an optional arm.</item>\\n<item>SPAG's exact reward structure (sparse vs dense, partial credit for taboo violations, dense vs sparse in Q20) \\u2014 supply-bound; the default sparse +1/0 is replaced by a one-parameter edit if the researcher supplies a value.</item>\\n<item>Whether SPAG's own published framing of 20-Question is adversarial or cooperative \\u2014 supply-bound; if adversarial, an additional sensitivity arm (Q20-as-zero-sum) is added at the researcher's discretion and the published contrast is reported with a framing discrepancy note in Section 11.</item>\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-76e02170-cfd9-4100-ae3b-8329f8ef3611", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 19}
